\par\par \b \uc2  Yamaguchi: Domestic Demand Fairly Weak, Capex Falling \b0\par\par\uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2002\par 21:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MONTREAL -(Dow Jones)- The Japanese economy shows signs of stabilizing, but weakness in consumer demand and persistent declines in capital spending will continue to constrain growth, Bank of Japan Deputy Governor Yutaka Yamaguchi said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "We believe economic activity as a whole is gradually getting stabilized," Yamaguchi said during a panel discussion at the International Monetary Conference in Montreal. He added that industrial production data also suggest that "corporate profits as well are beginning to improve." \par \par \uc2  He said consumers have shown relative resilience, even as labor market conditions have deteriorated, but noted that "domestic demand appears to be relatively weak. Capital spending is falling." \par \par \uc2  He said household spending and income are also falling and "it is reasonable to anticipate that it will be some time before consumption improves." \par \par \uc2  Yamaguchi said structural reform in Japan will take a long time. "Imposing constraints on government spending," he said, "is key to implementing reforms." Such constraints "help eliminate pork-barrel spending that's inefficient." \par \par \uc2  He added that it was extremely difficult under Japanese current financial conditions to arrive at a substantial tax reduction, but noted that even a modest tax reduction could help to demonstrate the government's determination to promote growth. Many analysts believe the government has hardly any ammunition to stimulate the economy via tax cuts. \par \par \uc2  Yamaguchi explained that the current monetary policy stance of the BOJ, characterized by so-called quantitative easing aimed at boosting liquidity in the Japanese banking system, is intended "to help ease deflationary pressure" as well as sustain economic growth." \par \par \uc2  "We believe these measures are in themselves quite powerful policy and that they have helped stabilize financial markets" against shocks emanating from a "fragile banking system." \par \par \uc2  Yamaguchi was speaking on a day in which the BOJ, acting on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was not formally announced by either the MOF or the BOJ and which was confirmed by a MOF official, was the fourth day of dollar-buying from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in two weeks. \par \par \uc2  He did not address exchange rate policy during the discussion, but Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge, who spoke before him, did, if only in theory, saying that central banks shouldn't try to manipulate exchange rates. "When a government or a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to hold an exchange rate...that distorts capital flows," he said, adding that "when we actively try to contain volatility...that on balance contributes to more volatility." He did not refer directly to the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the tone of his remarks clearly underscored the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor's well-known belief in freely floating exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  "The market itself works appropriately to contain volatility," Dodge said, adding that "individuals take positions knowing that they are betting against other market players and not the government." \par \par \uc2  Dodge said he didn't think \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility  has hampered world economic development or capital flows. \par \par \uc2  -By Michael Casey, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2009; michael.j.casey@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Michael Derby and Grainne McCarthy contributed to this article.) \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020604dy64009vp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
