\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar climbs off recent lows, U.S. data eyed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2002\par 14:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 3 (Reuters) - The dollar extended its recovery from last week's multi-month lows on Monday, supported by recent upbeat U.S. economic data and the threat of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold yen for dollars repeatedly on Friday - the third day in just over a week that it has sought to curb the yen's export-damaging strength - and a senior Japanese official warned \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels were still being closely watched. \par \par \uc2  Expectation of an upbeat U.S. manufacturing report later in the day was also supporting the greenback after surprisingly strong U.S. economic data on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "There's a feeling that U.S. manufacturing data out today could be reasonably strong," said Stacey Seltzer, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Brown Brothers Harriman. \par \par \uc2  "Since so much of the dollar's recent weakness has been related to U.S. recovery concerns, this is helping the dollar." \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit 15-month lows against the euro and six-month lows against the yen last Thursday as worries over the strength of the U.S. recovery dimmed the allure of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  The greenback has lost seven percent against both major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this quarter. \par \par \uc2  In holiday-thinned European trade, the greenback rose a third of a percent to $0.93 per euro and inched up to 124.20 yen . \par \par \uc2  London - the biggest centre for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - is closed on Monday and Tuesday to mark Queen Elizabeth's Golden Jubilee. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMIC DATA EYED \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Institute of Supply Management's May index of factory output is due at 1400 GMT. The index is expected to show a fourth straight month of growth, rising to 55.0, from 53.9 in April. \par \par \uc2  "The market will be closely watching both today's manufacturing data and U.S. unemployment data later in the week," said Seltzer. \par \par \uc2  "It could be a make-or-break week for the U.S. economy and the dollar." \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the Chicago Purchasing Management Index rose sharply to 60.8 in May, while U.S. workers productivity shot up 8.4 percent in the first quarter, its fastest pace in almost 19 years. \par \par \uc2  Analysts are also looking for strong Japanese growth figures for the January-March quarter from data due out late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed little reaction to data earlier in the day showing manufacturers in the euro zone enjoyed a second month of modest business growth. \par \par \uc2  The Reuters Eurozone Purchasing Managers' Index rose to 51.5 in May, the highest since February 2001, after edging above the 50 level that divides shrinkage from growth in April. \par \par \uc2  The index was broadly in line with the consensus forecast of 51.4. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone producer prices rose faster than expected in April, but analysts said the data did not shake their conviction that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep interest rates on hold for now. \par \par \uc2  CANADIAN FINANCE MINISTER OUSTED \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar fell to its lowest in over a week against the greenback after Canadian Prime Minister Jean Chretien fired veteran Finance Minister Paul Martin after a power struggle. \par \par \uc2  Martin was replaced by Deputy Prime Minister John Manley, who said there would be no change in economic policy. \par \par \uc2  Martin said he had full confidence in Manley, but dealers still took the opportunity to lock in profits on the Canadian unit's recent rise to nine-month highs. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar fell as low as 1.5380 against its U.S. counterpart but had recovered slightly to stand at 1.5350 by 1140 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "All the action was in the Far East," said a trader at a Canadian bank in Europe. "Because trading is so illiquid, the move was probably overdone." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020603dy6300okb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
