\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar on firmer ground, Japan threats support. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2002\par 12:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 3 (Reuters) - The dollar kept a firmer footing on Monday as recent upbeat U.S. economic data and the threat of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  made dealers reluctant to test last week's lows. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan sold yen for dollars repeatedly on Friday - the third day in just over a week that it has sought to curb the yen's export-damaging strength - and a senior Japanese official warned that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels were still being closely watched. \par \par \uc2  Expectation of an upbeat U.S. manufacturing report later in the day was also supporting the dollar, particularly after unexpectedly strong U.S. manufacturing and productivity data on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "There's a feeling that U.S. manufacturing data out today could be reasonably strong," said Stacey Seltzer, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Brown Brothers Harriman. \par \par \uc2  "Since so much of the dollar's recent weakness has been related to U.S. recovery concerns, this is helping the dollar." \par \par \uc2  A recovery in U.S. blue-chip stocks after Friday's upbeat data helped lift the greenback away from 15-month lows against the euro and six-month lows against the yen hit earlier in the week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was steady at 124.10 yen by the European midsession, holding more than a yen above last week's lows. It was also a touch firmer at $0.9320 to the euro. \par \par \uc2  A national holiday in the U.K. - the biggest European \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading centre - made for low volume trade. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMIC DATA EYED \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost seven percent against the euro and the yen since early April as worries over the strength of the U.S. economy have dimmed the once unrivalled appeal of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  "This week could be a make-or-break week for the U.S. economy and the dollar," added Seltzer. \par \par \uc2  "The market will be closely watching both today's manufacturing data and U.S. unemployment data later in the week." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Institute of Supply Management's May index of factory output is due at 1400 GMT. The index is expected to show a fourth straight month of growth, rising to 55.0, from 53.9 in April. An index reading above 50 indicates growth. \par \par \uc2  Teis Knuthsen, chief strategist at SEB in Stockholm said he expected the data to be strong, "pointing to a significant upturn in U.S. industrial production in the next couple of months." \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the Chicago Purchasing Management Index rose sharply to 60.8 in May, while U.S. workers productivity shot up 8.4 percent in the first quarter, its fastest pace in almost 19 years. \par \par \uc2  Analysts are also looking for strong Japanese growth figures for the January-March quarter from data due out late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed little reaction to data earlier in the day showing manufacturers in the euro zone enjoyed a second month of modest business growth. \par \par \uc2  The Reuters Eurozone Purchasing Managers' Index rose to 51.5 in May, the highest since February 2001, aftter edging above the 50 level that divides shrinkage from growth in April. \par \par \uc2  The index was broadly in line with the consensus forecast of 51.4. \par \par \uc2  CANADIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  CHIEF OUSTED \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar fell to its lowest in over a week against the greenback after Canadian Prime Minister Jean Chretien fired veteran Finance Minister Paul Martin after a power struggle. \par \par \uc2  Martin was replaced by Deputy Prime Minister John Manley, who said there would be no change in economic policy. \par \par \uc2  Martin said he had full confidence in Manley, but dealers still took the opportunity to lock in profits on the Canadian unit's recent rise to nine-month highs. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar traded at 1.5360 against its U.S. counterpart. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020603dy6300lb8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
