\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 1-Tokyo leads Asian bourses up, gold loses shine. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Neil Fullick \par \uc2 785 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2002\par 09:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 3 (Reuters) - A stable yen and hopes for positive Japan economic data later this week propelled Tokyo stocks higher on Monday while surprisingly strong U.S. leading indicators lifted other Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  However, gold lost its lustre as India and Pakistan seemed to strike a more conciliatory tone over the weekend, easing fears of a nuclear war erupting between the arch rivals. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar sank in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first reaction to Prime Minister Jean Chretien's sacking at the weekend of finance minister and long-term political rival Paul Martin. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the sacking could trigger a split in the party, especially if Martin challenged Chretien for the Liberal Party leadership next February. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar was quoted at C$1.5369 to the U.S. dollar at 0620 GMT against C$1.5278 on Friday after Canada posted a sizzling annualised 6.0 percent growth in first quarter gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  Repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to stop the yen's sharp rise brought relief to Tokyo shares. \par \par \uc2  Hopes that Friday's first-quarter Japanese GDP data would show the worst was over for the world's second biggest economy, which recorded three quarters of contraction in 2001, also bolstered sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The key Nikkei average closed 1.17 percent higher at 11,901.39. \par \par \uc2  Export earners Nissan Motor and Canon Inc firmed 4.31 percent and 2.93 percent respectively. Banks firmed on the GDP expectations, including the world's biggest bank by assets, Mizuho Holdings, which rose almost five percent. \par \par \uc2  GOLD SWAYS \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average ended 0.14 percent higher at 9,925.25 on Friday after data showed U.S. consumer sentiment in May was at its highest level in one and a half years and U.S. manufacturing orders posted their biggest gain in April since October. \par \par \uc2  The tech-led Nasdaq dropped one percent to 1,615.73. \par \par \uc2  Gold in Asia was quoted at $326.25 an ounce around 0625 GMT, down from New York's $326.65 on Friday, as the precious metal's fortunes moved in tandem with the rise and fall of tensions between nuclear rivals India and Pakistan. \par \par \uc2  Indian Defence Minister George Fernandes on Sunday played down the threat of nuclear war, telling Reuters "I don't think anyone should be worried about the nuclear thing." \par \par \uc2  His comments echoed those of Pakistan President Pervez Musharraf, who having described nuclear war as "unthinkable", said he was willing to establish a dialogue with Indian Prime Minister Atal Behari Vajpayee. \par \par \uc2  The two leaders are attending a 16-nation security conference in Kazakhstan where they are expected to face intense diplomatic pressure to defuse the standoff. \par \par \uc2  "That should restore some confidence in the market, though the undertone remains cautious," said Sandeep Jain of online broker Sharekhan.com. \par \par \uc2  At 0625 GMT, the benchmark 30-issue Bombay share index was up one percent at 3,159.73 points, while in Pakistan the Karachi 100 index was down 1.12 percent at 1,644.71. \par \par \uc2  In other Asian stock markets, Sydney's benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index closed steady at 3,375.6, with traders looking for the Reserve Bank of Australia to confirm on Tuesday expectations for a one quarter of a percentage point rise in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Australia's dollar was at 56.74 U.S. cents at 0642 GMT, down from Friday's 16-month peak of 56.97 cents, which had followed hawkish comments by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Ian Macfarlane. \par \par \uc2  AMPLE GASOLINE \par \par \uc2  Korea's Composite Stock Price Index gained 1.07 percent to 804.93 points, while Taiwan's key TAIEX index shed 1.84 percent to close at 5,571.08. \par \par \uc2  At 0630 GMT, Singapore's Straits Times Index was up 0.17 percent at 1,674.69 points in quiet trading, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was up 0.64 percent at 11,374.12 points. \par \par \uc2  One Singapore dealer said slow market conditions were possibly due to the World Cup soccer under way in South Korea and Japan this month. \par \par \uc2  "Maybe part of the punting has been diverted to football, which seems more exciting than this market," he said. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices were lower on Monday despite weekend OPEC comments that the group would maintain a tight rein on supplies into the third quarter. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the fall reflected concerns about ample gasoline stocks in the United States, the world's biggest energy consumer, indicating weak demand as the peak driving season started. \par \par \uc2  At 0632 GMT, benchmark NYMEX July crude futures were down 15 cents at $25.16 per barrel. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020603dy6300fr4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
