\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar finds support on U.S. data, BOJ fears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2002\par 08:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 3 (Reuters) - The dollar found support against the yen on Monday after strong U.S. economic data and on lingering fears of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  As of 0555 GMT, the dollar was at 124.23 yen little changed from 124.09 in late U.S. trade on Friday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market a number of times on Friday to sell yen for dollars, the third \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities in just over a week. \par \par \uc2  Some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurred late in the Tokyo evening, but dealers said there was no sign that the BOJ acted through banks in New York. They suggested it had placed orders with overnight desks in major commercial banks in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "I think they want to prevent the yen from breaking the 124 level," said Noriyuki Kato, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  section at ING Barings in Tokyo. "We are not in the mood to challenge further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least in Tokyo." \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials kept up their warnings with the Finance Ministry's Haruhiko Kuroda saying he was watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carefully. \par \par \uc2  The top financial diplomat added that he had heard there was no change in the United States' strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar's rise was expected to be limited, especially with strong yen-buying offers from Japanese exporters seen around 124.50 and 125 yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Though it has got more difficult to sell the dollar, there is no change in the fact that its topside is still heavily weighed," a dealer at a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  Trading was expected to remain quiet with the London market closed for holidays on Monday and Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN GDP, ECB IN FOCUS \par \par \uc2  The dollar also firmed against the euro, supported by strong U.S. manufacturing and productivity data released on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Chicago Purchasing Management Index rose to 60.8 in May, far above the expected 54.9. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at 93.13 cents against late New York's 93.34. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 115.65 against 115.88 yen in late New York. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market's next focus would be Japan's release of first-quarter 2002 gross domestic product (GDP) data on Friday, which many expect to be strong. \par \par \uc2  "I think it will give a further boost to the yen but the rise will be capped due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears," a Japanese city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Bot some were more sceptical. \par \par \uc2  "I think the expectations are overblown," said Marshall Gittler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  In the near term, traders will be looking at the manufacturing index from the Institute for Supply Management due later in the day. It is expected to come in at 54.1 in May, up from 53.9 in April. \par \par \uc2  For the euro, the next focus will be the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council meeting scheduled for Thursday, with dealers noting some speculation that it could raise interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think there will be any change, though some have said there could be some, especially after the recent rise in the euro," said ING Barings' Kato. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar eased against the U.S. dollar after Canadian Prime Minister Jean Chretien fired veteran Finance Minister Paul Martin after a power struggle. \par \par \uc2  Martin was replaced by Deputy Prime Minister John Manley, who said there would be no change in economic policy. Martin said he had full confidence in Manley, but the Canadian dollar fell to around C$1.5362 from about C$1.5278 in late New York. \par \par \uc2  Martin is widely credited with engineering the recovery of the Canadian economy after the Liberals took power in 1993. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020603dy6300d4n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
