\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr/Yen Edges Up As MOF Keeps Market On Guard \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jonathan Soble \par \uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2002\par 09:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar edged slightly higher in subdued trading in Asia Monday as fears of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities caused traders to think twice about buying yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was capped, however, by steady selling by Japanese exporters concerned about dollar weakness in the longer term and hedging their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been sold off across the board in recent weeks and continues to suffer against the yen amid worries over the performance of U.S. stocks and uncertainty about the sustainability of the U.S. economic recovery, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Recent aggressive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  have done little to dispel the underlying bearish mood towards dollar, but they have helped to put a floor under the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With the threat of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  below Y124 hanging over the market, players were reluctant to sell the dollar aggressively. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Vice Finance Minster for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda kept fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alive Monday by warning traders that the ministry was keeping a close eye on exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  "We are watching market movements very closely," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  With the dollar hugging the Y124.35 mark for most of the session, traders said they don't anticipate a new round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at current levels. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities seem content to have halted the dollar's downward arc, they said, and were unlikely to try and push it into a renewed upswing. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar should trade sideways within a Y123.00-Y125.50 range this week, as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bias is now neutral," said a Japanese city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The day of relative calm comes on the heels of Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sold yen on behalf of the Finance Ministry for the third time since late May to stem the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent rise against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (1:15 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y124.33 a tad higher than Y124.32 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at Y124.40 late Friday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.9315, lower than $0.9318 late Friday in New York. The euro was at $0.9373 in Tokyo Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y115.85, up from Y115.84 in New York late Friday. The euro was at Y116.60 in Tokyo late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The ministry spent an estimated $8.5 billion on its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday, much more than most observers had believed Japanese authorities were willing to spend. \par \par \uc2  The size of the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bolstered the view that \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials are serious about backing up their tough talk on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates with action. \par \par \uc2  Amid generally flat near-term forecasts for the dollar/yen, some analysts say the dollar could strengthen depending on the results of key economic data to be released this week. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, Japan's Cabinet office will release nationwide gross domestic product data for the January-March quarter. The data is expected to show strong growth of 6.8% annualized, according to a consensus of economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  However, this forecast could be revised downward if capital spending figures due out Wednesday in a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  corporate survey show signs of continued weakness, said Morgan Stanley \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Toru Umemoto. \par \par \uc2  He added that even if the GDP data are as strong as expected, the market may be concerned that the data are inflated - a charge that has dogged the government for years. \par \par \uc2  "Therefore, now, the risk is to the downside" for the economy, Umemoto said, adding that disappointing data could push the dollar to Y128 in the coming week or two. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.4561, up slightly from $1.4558 in New York, but lower than $1.4660 in Tokyo Friday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par jonathan.soble@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The chart of late Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates that accompanied Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par been replaced with an enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixture that is updated\par hourly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Subscribers can find this table:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.\par \f28