\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View: Dollar Could Remain Under Wall Of Pressure \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 1,136 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2002\par 00:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Already bruised from a beating over the past month, the dollar is set to remain on the backfoot this week, trapped in a fog of geopolitical tensions and growing concerns about the timing of a convincing rebound for U.S. corporate earnings. \par \par \uc2  In a week when several of the world's major central banks are due to meet - with at least two expected to raise interest rates - the dollar will also remain vulnerable to shifts in capital towards countries perceived as further along in the growth curve than the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "Credible rate hikes are being rewarded in the market," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in New York. \par \par \uc2  Alongside the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonanza, markets will also be buffeted by a wave of heavyweight data, of which the highlights will be non-farm payrolls and the unemployment rate for May. The U.S. Institute for Supply Management's May index Monday should also help cast more light on the resilience of the U.S. rebound. \par \par \uc2  Whether the dollar will be able to garner much support from encouraging U.S data is unclear, however. Over the past few sessions, the greenback has seemed stuck in a wave of bearish sentiment, appearing somewhat immune to positive economic reports. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  did manage to rally in the wake of a much better-than-expected manufacturing report from the Chicago Purchasing Management Index, as well as healthy consumer sentiment data from the University of Michigan, but the gains seemed tentative at best. \par \par \uc2  "It's going to be about the same things this week but to a new level," said Rebecca Patterson, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York. "India and Pakistan will continue to be a major focus and the U.S. data will be a dominant factor." \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the market remains vulnerable to developments in the Subcontinent, which have driven safe-haven buying of the classic hedges in times of global uncertainty: gold and the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  In a week full of potential dollar-sensitive events, market watchers will also keep a close eye out for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times in the past two weeks to weaken the yen - as well as hints of any nuanced changes to the prevailing U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, often termed the strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  While there have certainly been fundamental factors behind the dollar's fall to around 16-month lows against the euro, six-month troughs compared to the yen and multi-month lows against many of its other counterparts, part of the decline has also stemmed from a sense that the Bush administration doesn't object to a weaker dollar, as long as it falls gradually and doesn't spark a stampede out of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with The Wall Street Journal last week, Lawrence Lindsey, head of the White House National Economic Council, only reinforced this view, indicating that the U.S. still supports a strong dollar policy and that the dollar is "strong," right now. \par \par \uc2  His comments "make it clear that the administration...is not concerned about the dollar's recent drops," said Anne Parker Mills, an economist at Brown Brothers Harriman, in New York. "That view reinforces the market's growing bearishness on the dollar." \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the comments back up a general view in the market that the U.S. Treasury won't act independently, or with its Group of Seven partners, to prop up the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "We are nowhere near close to levels where the U.S. or G7 might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the dollar," said Stewart, at Morgan Stanley. "The dollar is still strong in the context of how much it had appreciated. It's unwinding strength, rather than collapsing." \par \par \uc2  Still, investors will pay close attention to speeches by Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill and Treasury Under Secretary John Taylor in Washington on Wednesday for any more hints on dollar policy. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan is also due to take part in a panel discussion on Tuesday in Montreal, although he's not scheduled to give a speech. \par \par \uc2  Market participants, meanwhile, entertain no such doubts about Japan's monetary authorities. Concerned that a rallying yen might derail the country's fragile cyclical recovery, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  - acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on three separate days in the past two weeks, stepping in to buy dollars in both European and New York trading Friday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had some degree of success, but the BOJ's efforts are clearly limited by the fact that they're running in to a wall of broad dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  "I do not think they're trying to change the trend, but I do think they're very concerned about how much yen strength there is," said Patterson, at JP Morgan. "They don't want it to threaten their exporters." \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the BOJ to enter the market again this week, with some even suggesting - with a degree of amusement - that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might take advantage of the fact that many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers will be distracted by the World Cup, which kicked off Friday. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the dollar was at Y124.32, off its highs for the session, but more than a full yen higher than Y123.27 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $0.9336, down from $0.9376 late Thursday in New York. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.5726, up from CHF1.5605 late Thursday in New York. Sterling was at $1.4558, down from $1.4648 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Also set to grab the market's attention is a meeting of the euro group of finance ministers in Luxembourg on Monday, to discuss the rise of the euro and whether it threatens to derails its recovery. The euro's nearly 10% rise against the dollar since February means European exports won't be nearly as price competitive as they have been in the past, a matter that is sure to be of more concern to European policy makers. \par \par \uc2  On the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  menu, the Reserve Bank of Australia and Bank of Canada - both of which meet Tuesday  - are expected to tighten policy as they grapple with roaring economies and seek to keep inflation under control. The monetary policy boards of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank of England and Sweden's Riksbank also meet this week. Most analysts expect the former and latter to keep interest rates unchanged, but some market watchers don't rule out a rate increase from the BoE. \par \par \uc2  -Grainne McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020020602dy62000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
