\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Gains on Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Strong Data \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2002\par 05:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar rallied Friday, cheered by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen in both the European and New York sessions, leaving the greenback trading higher against most of its widely-traded rivals. \par \par \uc2  Stronger-than-expected U.S. economic data also paved the way for an advance of some U.S. equities and helped lift the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Purchasing Management Association of Chicago index of area business activity rose to 60.8 in May on a seasonally adjusted basis from 54.7 in April, indicating expansion and the first time the index was above 60 since April 1999. \par \par \uc2  The more robust-than-expected report was viewed as quite positive for the U.S. economic outlook -- contrasting with some mixed economic data from Europe -- and helped drive a better bid for the dollar, said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Barings Capital Markets in New York. \par \par \uc2  Market participants also remained reluctant to push the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stronger after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Asian, European and North American trading. \par \par \uc2  A Japanese government source in the U.S. confirmed to Dow Jones Newswires that the ministry had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the New York morning, buying dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  That's the first time the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the New York session this year to tame the rallying yen. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in Friday's global session, the BOJ bought dollars for yen in an effort to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as traders in Europe were starting their day, pushing the dollar up over a full yen to a post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  high around 124.60 yen. \par \par \uc2  Confirming the dollar purchases, Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said in a statement that "we have taken appropriate action today in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as a follow-up to last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," adding that "we will continue to closely monitor the market and take appropriate action as necessary." \par \par \uc2  But even though the dollar had certainly rocketed initially after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the effects proved relatively short-lived. By the time New York markets were gearing up in the early morning, the dollar had retrenched again to a level of 124.01 yen at 7:40 a.m. EDT. \par \par \uc2  The next flurry of apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities, which caused the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spike up to around 124.40 yen, occurred at around 10 a.m. EDT, according to some bankers and the Japanese government official. \par \par \uc2  "We believe they did do a light amount this morning," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in New York. "The types of names in the market were indicative of the MOF." \par \par \uc2  That followed the release of stronger-than-expected Chicago manufacturing data in the U.S. and occurred when the dollar was, in any case, enjoying a bit of a rally. \par \par \uc2  Last week, the BOJ did two rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , one during Asian dealings and the other in European trading time. Traders estimate the combined total of all three \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  thus far ranges between about $10 billion and $15 billion. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts had doubted initial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports -- and indeed some New York analysts remain unconvinced that the Bank of Japan has stepped in during the New York day -- largely because the jump in the dollar proved fairly short-lived, but also due to the finance ministry's lack of an immediate official confirmation of the move. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the dollar was at 124.32 yen, well off its highs of the session, but more than a full yen higher than 123.27 yen late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.9318, down from $0.9376 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.5726 Swiss francs, up from 1.5605 francs late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.4558, down from $1.4648 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Among other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Canadian dollar finished close to the fresh 10-month highs against the U.S. dollar it had reached earlier in Friday's session. The dollar was at $C1.5275 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires; john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096 \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020601dy61000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
