\par\par \b \uc2  Last Week's BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Rears Its Head In Fed Data \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael S. Derby \par \uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2002\par 20:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- A number of economists now believe a significant portion of last week's Bank Of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was invested in U.S. Treasury and agency debt securities. \par \par \uc2  And that suggests that additional dollar purchases by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday that analysts estimated to be in the range of $3 billion to $8 billion could end up in the same place. \par \par \uc2  As evidence, economists are pointing to a fairly large swing in a component of Federal Reserve's weekly money supply data, released on Thursday, that showed that the institution's holdings of securities for foreign central banks shot up $8.7 billion for the week. \par \par \uc2  Those so-called custody holdings are comprised mostly of Treasurys, and to a lesser degree agency securities, and the Fed does not elaborate on reasons behind swings. \par \par \uc2  Even so, the swing in the data is well beyond what would normally have happened in a week where Treasury supply changes were not an issue, and compares with normal weekly moves that can range between $3 billion to $4 billion, in either the plus or minus column. \par \par \uc2  The custody holdings move was "a huge number. BoJ, is what I say," said William Sullivan, money market economist with Morgan Stanley in Jersey City, N.J. Indeed, the heft of the increase "reflects the process of last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " as the Japanese officials sought a parking place to put the dollars they'd bought while trying to force the yen lower, he explained. \par \par \uc2  Treasury market participants have for some time been on the lookout for signs the BoJ would take its newly acquired dollars and buy Treasurys, most likely in the shorter end of the market. The BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday and Thursday of last week - to the tune of an estimated $5 billion to $6 billion - and given the settlement issues, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars would have been the biggest wild card in the Fed's money supply data. \par \par \uc2  "By the weekend it's likely the Bank of Japan would have needed to acquire some securities to invest the dollars," said John Youngdahl, economist at Goldman Sachs in New York. He added that Treasury supply levels, which are the main factor that drive big swings in custody holdings reports, should not have been a factor. \par \par \uc2  While general concerns about terrorism and the possibility of war between India and Pakistan, among other factors, have been lending an overarching safe harbor bid to Treasurys, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also brought additional support to the market, mostly from traders who've hoped the BoJ money was heading their way. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ usually puts its dollars to work in the front end of the curve, mainly Treasury bills,"  said Gerald Lucas, senior government bond strategist at Merrill Lynch in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Foggy Notion\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Given the fogginess over the size of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par economists are hesitant to say what the custody holding changes indicate\par about the size of last week's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But some were willing to\par say the magnitude of the change was consistent with estimates in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that totaled between $5 billion and $6\par billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some market participants said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week may have been\par an important factor driving what was a very hot two-year note sale - an\par offering much stronger than had been expected. They said that it appears\par the BOJ may have been a big buyer at the sale, as it again sought a\par place to park it greenbacks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Now, with Friday's apparently even larger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , economists\par will look to next Thursday's data on the Fed's custody holdings to flesh\par out how big the latest move was and where the dollars went.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yet the latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't appear to have had a noticeable\par effect on a government bond market that was being bloodied Friday by a\par series of positive economic data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sharon Stark, fixed-income strategist at Legg Mason, in Baltimore,\par said: "I'm surprised there wasn't more talk of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " among\par Treasury market participants. What happens with those dollars will be an\par issue, and "the inflow from the Japanese in terms of the short-end will\par be significant," she said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Michael S. Derby, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4192;\par michael.derby@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Michael Mackenzie contributed to this report)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020531dy5v00dfd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
