\par\par \b \uc2  Yen braves Moody's, still vigilant on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2002\par 07:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 31 (Reuters) - The yen held firm near its six-month high against the dollar on Friday, braving the embarrassing but long-expected downgrade of Japan's sovereign rating by Moody's. \par \par \uc2  Still, the yen's rise was kept in check by wariness that Japanese monetary authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to stem its after they did so twice last week at a weaker level. \par \par \uc2  Shortly after noon in Tokyo (0300 GMT), Moody's Investors Service said it cut its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating on Japanese government debt by two notches to A2 from Aa3, putting Japan far behind all other major industrialised countries, which have a rating of triple A or double A at worst. \par \par \uc2  The decision was within but at the largest end of market expectations, as Moody's had said in the past the results of its review on Japan could be between no change to a two-notch downgrade. \par \par \uc2  The dollar initially rose a little after the announcement, to 123.40/50 yen from 123.25 yen. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback soon lost its momentum and slid to around 123.00 yen, below its late U.S. levels around 123.25 and just shy of its six-month low of 122.82. \par \par \uc2  "The Moody's impact was as short-lived as a firework," said Toshiyuki Aoyagi, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nissho Iwai Financial Link. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yen was supported not only because the downgrade had long been expected but also because the rating firm placed its rating outlook on Japan at stable, suggesting there will be no more rate cuts on Japan for some time. \par \par \uc2  Vincent Truglia, managing director of Moody's, told Reuters there was less than a 50 percent chance that Japan's rating would go on review again in the next 12 to 24 months. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market is now more dominated by worries about the U.S. economy rather than Japan, and that the dollar's near-term outlook was bleak. The dollar fell nearly eight percent both against the euro and the yen in the last two months. \par \par \uc2  FOCUS BACK ON \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  With concerns over the pace of a U.S. recovery, its ballooning current account deficit, and to top it all off a poor performance on Wall Street, the euro rose to 93.98/03 cents in late afternoon, up from 93.70 in late U.S. trade, closing in on its 16-month peak of 94.15 recorded the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also slightly firmer against the yen at 115.67/78 yen compared to around 115.50 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  As for dollar/yen, dealers are now solely obsessed with the possibility of Japanese monetary authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although Japanese officials did not specify what \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  it sold the yen against, dealers believe the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice last weak to curb the export-damaging yen's rise. It had bought at levels around 124 yen - about a full yen above where it is now. \par \par \uc2  "I think the dollar will continue to fall gradually, unless the BOJ steps into the market," said Takashi Toyahara, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities. \par \par \uc2  Most dealers think the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a certainty if the dollar falls below the latest low of 122.82. \par \par \uc2  However, traders noted that market conditions on Friday were not ideally suited for such a move. \par \par \uc2  "The market is long of dollars, thus it is not an ideal time for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step in," said Shuzo Kakuta, senior deputy general manager at Mizuho Corporate Bank. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by monetary authorities is most effective when short positions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are piled up in the market. Under such conditions, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could prompt short-covering, boosting upward momentum in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  A series of economic indicators released this morning was in line with market forecasts and with views that Japanese economy is heading towards a cyclical recovery. \par \par \uc2  Average spending of wage earners rose a real 0.9 percent in April from a year earlier. The ratio of job offers to applicants rose to slightly to 0.52. \par \par \uc2  "The rise of the job-offers-to-applicants ratio is kind of encouraging news. It is a typical coincident indicator and moves in line with the recovery of industrial production," said Takehiro Sato, economist, at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020601dy5v009kj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
