\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar propped up by repeated  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2002\par 12:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 31 (Reuters) - The dollar jumped against the yen as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, fearing its recent strength would damage the nation's tentative export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  The greenback - which sank to six-month lows below 123 yen on Thursday - shot up more than one percent to around 124.60 after the first action in late Asian trade, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's third day of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in less than two weeks. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in early European trade and also half way through the European morning, and dealers estimate it bought around $5-6 billion in total on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  played the market very well... They can continue to play the market but the political environment won't support it. What they can do is to slow the ascent," said Stacey Seltzer, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Brown Brothers Harriman. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen over seven percent both against the euro and the yen in the last two months as doubts over the pace of the U.S. recovery have dimmed the once unrivalled appeal of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was struggling to hold its post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains and had fallen back below 124 yen by 0940 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "(Japan's) determination matches the determination they showed in September. On that occasion they spent the quivalent of 27 billion dollars," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  Banking sources estimate the BOJ spent around 1.067 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in two days of action last week. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was also trading within sight of the previous session's 15-month lows beyond $0.94 against the euro and 2-1/2 year lows against the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  WEATHERS DOWNGRADE \par \par \uc2  The euro also vaulted one percent to 116.65 yen after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - its highest level in two weeks - before drifting back to 116.20. \par \par \uc2  Before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen was holding firm, taking a two-notch downgrade of Japan's sovereign rating by Moody's Investors Service in its stride. \par \par \uc2  Moody's cut its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating on Japanese government debt by two notches to A2, putting Japan far behind all other major industrialised countries. \par \par \uc2  The dollar initially rose a little after the announcement, but soon shed its gains. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the impact was muted not only because it had been widely expected but also because the outlook on the new rating was stable, suggesting no further cuts for some time. \par \par \uc2  Vincent Truglia, a managing director at Moody's, told Reuters there was less than a 50 percent chance that Japan's rating would go on review again in the next 12 to 24 months. \par \par \uc2  US CONCERNS DOMINANT \par \par \uc2  The Wall Street Journal online edition on Friday quoted U.S. President George W. Bush's chief economic advisor Lawrence Lindsey as saying the U.S. still favours a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and playing down the latest drop in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market is now more dominated by worries about the U.S. economy rather than Japan, and that the dollar's near-term outlook was bleak. \par \par \uc2  U.S. data including factory orders, the University of Michigan sentiment survey and the Chicago Purchasing Managers' Index are all due later in the day and will be closely watched for clues on the strength of the U.S. recovery. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were casting a wary eye on U.S. stocks, which have shown persistent weakness in recent weeks due to worries about corporate profits and accounting practices. On Friday U.S. stock futures were pointing to a firmer opening on Wall Street, giving some relief to dollar bulls. \par \par \uc2  The market has U.S. ISM purchasing managers' data for May and Japanese gross domestic product for the first quarter next week. \par \par \uc2  "Next week will be a big data week and key for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Anticipation of soft data means there is a possibility the market could push the dollar yet lower, at least against the euro," Seltzer said. \par \par \uc2  A flash estimate of euro zone consumer prices data showed inflation running at 2.0 percent on the year in May, compared with 2.4 percent in April, falling to match the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target ceiling of two percent. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone M3 money supply data showed a surprising rise to 7.5 percent in April from 7.3 previously but analysts said they expected it to have little immediate impact on interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020531dy5v00lgc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
