\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar bounces versus yen as  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2002\par 10:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 31 (Reuters) - The dollar jumped against the yen on Friday as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fearing its recent rise would harm the nation's tentative export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  The greenback - which sank to six-month lows below 123 yen on Thursday - shot up more than one percent to 124.60 after the action, the Bank of Japan's third foray into the market in less than two weeks. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the largest pool of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in the world, but dealers said it would have its work cut out fighting a broad-based trend of dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  "It looks as though the Bank of Japan was more aggressive than last week, but it will have a tough job fighting the trend," said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment towards the dollar remains extremely bearish while there is growing optimism that Japan's economy is on the mend." \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen over seven percent both against the euro and the yen in the last two months as doubts over the pace of the U.S. recovery have dimmed the once unrivalled appeal of U.S. assets. The dollar was struggling to hold its post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains in the European session and had fallen back below 124 yen by 0745 GMT. \par \par \uc2  It was also trading within sight of the previous session's 15-month lows beyond $0.94 against the euro and 2-1/2 year lows against the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  MOODY'S DOWNGRADE \par \par \uc2  The euro also vaulted one percent to 116.65 yen after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - its highest level in two weeks - before drifting back to 116.20. \par \par \uc2  Before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen was holding firm, taking a two-notch downgrade of Japan's sovereign rating by Moody's Investors Service in its stride. \par \par \uc2  Moody's cut its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating on Japanese government debt by two notches to A2, putting Japan far behind all other major industrialised countries. \par \par \uc2  The dollar initially rose a little after the announcement, but soon shed its gains. \par \par \uc2  "The Moody's impact was as short-lived as a firework," said Toshiyuki Aoyagi, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nissho Iwai Financial Link. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the impact was muted not only because it had been widely expected but also because the outlook on the new rating was stable, suggesting no further cuts for some time. \par \par \uc2  Vincent Truglia, a managing director at Moody's, told Reuters there was less than a 50 percent chance that Japan's rating would go on review again in the next 12 to 24 months. \par \par \uc2  US CONCERNS DOMINANT \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market is now more dominated by worries about the U.S. economy rather than Japan, and that the dollar's near-term outlook was bleak. \par \par \uc2  U.S. data including factory orders, the University of Michigan sentiment survey and the Chicago Purchasing Managers' Index are all due later in the day and will be closely watched for clues on the strength of the U.S. recovery. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone money supply and consumer price data are due later in the session. \par \par \uc2  Japanese data released earlier reinforced the view that Japan's economy is heading towards a cyclical recovery. \par \par \uc2  Average spending of wage earners rose a real 0.9 percent in April from a year earlier, while the ratio of job offers to applicants rose to slightly to 0.52. \par \par \uc2  "The rise of the job-offers-to-applicants ratio is kind of encouraging news. It is a typical coincident indicator and moves in line with the recovery of industrial production," said Takehiro Sato, an economist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  Japan's jobless rate held steady in April at 5.2 percent, slightly better than expected, but consumer prices fell for a 31st month, underscoring the battle to curb deflation in the world's second-largest economy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020531dy5v00gin\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
