\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 4-Moody's surprises with two-notch cut of Japan. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 1,038 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2002\par 11:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 31 (Reuters) - Brushing aside government protests and signs Japan's economy is on the mend, Moody's Investors Service on Friday cut Japan's long-term credit rating by a bigger-than-expected two notches, citing debt levels it said were unprecedented in the postwar era in the developed world. \par \par \uc2  Moody's cut Japan's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating to A2 from Aa3 but kept its long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt rating at Aa1. \par \par \uc2  It said the outlook for both ratings was stable. \par \par \uc2  Japan's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating was already the lowest among major industrialised nations after an upgrading of Italy to Aa2. \par \par \uc2  The two-notch cut is a setback for the government, which has been railing for weeks against what it sees as unfair treatment of the world's second-biggest economy by rating agencies. \par \par \uc2  The scale of the downgrade took most in the markets by surprise, but reaction was muted. Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa was dismissive of the Moody's decision. \par \par \uc2  "They're doing it for business. Just because they do such things we won't change our policies," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "The market doesn't seem to be paying attention," he added. \par \par \uc2  His righthand man, Haruhiko Kuroda, was more blunt. \par \par \uc2  "The markets may be ignoring Moody's rating, but we will not accept such an unjust and incorrect rating decision," said Kuroda, the vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  "We strongly request Moody's review their decision." \par \par \uc2  The yen dipped on the news, then rallied - prompting the Bank of Japan to come piling into the market to drive it lower, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen. A strong yen threatens \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tentative export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  At 0910 GMT the yen was trading around 124.05 to the dollar after the Bank of Japan drove the dollar as high as 124.60. The dollar sank to six-month lows below 123 yen on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Tokyo stock market ended flat and government bonds also proved resilient, with the market relieved that the long-awaited Moody's factor was finally out of the way. \par \par \uc2  Market analysts and economists said it was unlikely there would be a significant market impact. \par \par \uc2  Japan has largely been immune to repeated cuts in its credit standing because its debt is entirely denominated in yen and is mostly owed to domestic investors, who see it as a safe haven alternative to risky stocks or deposits in shaky banks. \par \par \uc2  POSITIVE FACTOR \par \par \uc2  One positive factor for the markets was a statement by Moody's managing director Vincent Truglia, who told Reuters a further review was unlikely in the next one or two years. \par \par \uc2  But controversy raged. \par \par \uc2  "I think the biggest issue from this downgrade will be trust in Moody's as a ratings agency," said Takuji Aida, fixed income strategist at Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  Added Peter Morgan, senior economist at HSBC Securities: "It's a fundamental question - what do ratings really mean?" \par \par \uc2  With the change, Moody's assigns to Japan - the world's biggest creditor and holder of the biggest pile of foreign reserves - the same "upper medium" local rating as Cyprus, Greece, Israel, Latvia, Poland and South Africa. \par \par \uc2  It is also now ranked lower than Botswana, something that will irk Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, who grumbled recently about the "nonsense" of Japan's "national strength" being rated lower than countries to which it provides aid. \par \par \uc2  Brushing aside the government's criticism that Japan's rating was already too low, Moody's said its decision "reflects the conclusion that the Japanese government's current and anticipated economic policies will be insufficient to prevent continued deterioration in Japan's domestic debt position". \par \par \uc2  Keeping up the pressure on the agencies, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said it wanted a better explanation from Standard and Poor's on why it cut Japan's long-term local and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ratings by one notch to AA-minus last month. The new Moody's local rating is two notches below S&P's rating. \par \par \uc2  Unhappy with three downgrades in six months, Japan took the unprecedented step this month of writing to the big three agencies asking them to explain and justify their decisions. \par \par \uc2  The decision to downgrade comes when it appears Japan's economy is bottoming out after three quarters of decline, thanks to a rise in exports into the recovering U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  NARROW RECOVERY \par \par \uc2  Truglia agreed Japan appeared to be experiencing a mild economic upturn, but he said the rebound was narrowly based. \par \par \uc2  "What we are more interested in is how sustainable any upturn might be," he said. \par \par \uc2  In its announcement, Moody's said the downgrade reflected its conclusion the government's policies would be insufficient to prevent a continued deterioration in Japan's debt position. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's general government indebtedness, however measured, will approach levels unprecedented in the postwar era in the developed world, and as such Japan will be entering 'uncharted territory'," the ratings agency said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's public debt will stand at more than 140 percent of gross domestic product by the end of the current fiscal year in March 2003 - more than double that of the United States. But Moody's said there was little risk of a financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  "The essentially closed nature of the Japanese financial system, in which household deposits finance bank and postal savings claims on the government, provides a high level of financial stability such that the risk of an uncontrolled funding crisis is remote," the agency said. \par \par \uc2  The single-A rating could cause longer term problems, however. Some financial institutions shy away, or are outright prevented, from holding debt rated below AA, and Bank for International Settlements rules to be implemented in 2005 would remove the risk-free status of bonds in the single-A category. \par \par \uc2  Japan's foreign reserves stood at a record $407 billion in April. Reserves held by the 12-nation euro zone and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stood at a combined $254 billion at end-February. \par \par \uc2  Also, policy-makers note, Japan has the world's largest pile of personal savings - equivalent to about $11 trillion, or about $87,000 for every man, woman and child in the country. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020531dy5v007pt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
