\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits multi-month lows vs yen and euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2002\par 13:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 30 (Reuters) - The dollar took another beating on Thursday, hitting a 15-month low against the euro and a six-month low against the yen, weighed by persistent doubts about the U.S. recovery and the waning appeal of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar was lifted off its lows against the yen by nervousness that Japan could sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again as it did last week. It also recovered from 28-month lows against the Swiss franc after the Swiss National Bank trimmed its repo rate yet again. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks fell again on Wednesday as corporate profit warnings and accounting practice concerns soured sentiment. The Dow Jones industrial average has lost three percent in the past fortnight, the tech-heavy Nasdaq twice that. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment on the dollar remains negative. We believe it is equity-related. The Dow has been under pressure," said Neil Parker, market strategist at RBS Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose as far as $0.9393 before easing back a third of a cent by 1112 GMT, still up a quarter percent on the day. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen more than five percent against the dollar this year and nearly four cents in May alone. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said heavy selling by U.S. investment banks pushed the dollar down to a six-month low of 123.45 yen before it shot up half a yen within seconds on wariness that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , opposed to export-damaging yen strength, could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had no comment on talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and dealers said they had not detected the hand of the BOJ. The dollar was still down more than half a percent against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also dipped against the Swiss franc to 1.5633 before rising slightly when the Swiss National Bank cut its repo rate another two basis points to 0.99 percent in an effort to curb franc strength. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR GLOOM \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was still very wary of the possibility of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  even though BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves should be left to the market. \par \par \uc2  Japan stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Wednesday and Thursday last week after the dollar fell as far as 123.50 yen. Tokyo is worried that the yen's rise, up six percent this year, could damage the country's tentative export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  "There is still a lot of nervousness that the BOJ will come in below the 123.50 level," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said investors were also still concerned that U.S. jobless claims due later could stay above 400,000, suggesting the May payrolls report next week might disappoint. \par \par \uc2  "It's general dollar pessimism at the moment," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020530dy5u00nrx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
