\par\par \b \uc2  TOCOM gold hardly moves as players snap up profits. \b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2002\par 10:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 30 (Reuters) - Yen-based gold futures scarcely budged on Thursday, ending narrowly mixed as profit-taking and a firmer yen sapped momentum from bullion's safe-haven charge to new bull market highs. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark April 2003 contract settled down one yen per gram at 1,291 while other months ranged from one yen down to two yen up. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar capped the market," one broker said. "Speculators are also liquidating their positions for profit-taking." \par \par \uc2  Gold futures on the Tokyo Commodity Exchange hit levels not seen in almost two months on Wednesday after bullion barnstormed above $325 in New York. \par \par \uc2  By some measures, gold's watershed 2002 advance has now exceeded highs from the panicky short-covering spike after the September 1999 Washington Accord curbing \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales. \par \par \uc2  But little of gold's new vigour was evident on a sluggish TOCOM on Thursday, as investors kept their eyes fixed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where the yen edged back up toward levels that prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  Turnover of TOCOM gold clocked in at 76,518 lots or 76.5 tonnes, down from Wednesday's 104,643 lots. \par \par \uc2  Open interest stood at 376,598 lots as of the end of Wednesday, up from 373,608 lots on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fetched 124.18/23 yen just after the close of TOCOM trade, against 124.52/53 at the same time on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  A firmer Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has helped to keep yen-based gold prices fairly stable in recent trade, offsetting gains in the dollar-based spot price. \par \par \uc2  "It's been staying in the range for the last month - let's say between 1,250 and 1,300 yen," a trader said, noting muted buying interest from Japanese investors despite the metal's allure as a safe haven in rocky times. \par \par \uc2  "The public and the end-users need a price change." \par \par \uc2  Japan's gold imports fell 57.2 percent month-on-month in April to 5.56 tonnes, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Wednesday. That level was still a 76.5 percent jump from the year before. \par \par \uc2  Gold's so-called "war commodity" status has seen its price rise 17 percent this year, most recently in response to mounting tensions between nuclear rivals Pakistan and India. \par \par \uc2  In the latest news out of the Indian subcontinent, two militants stormed an Indian police camp in disputed Kashmir, killing three policemen, as Washington warned that extremists could turn the standoff into a regional war. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold was trading at $324.30/324.80 an ounce at 0630 GMT, down slightly from $325.50/326.00 last quoted in New York. \par \par \uc2  Platinum futures ended mixed after a day of consolidation. \par \par \uc2  Benchmark April 2003 lost 10 yen per gram to 2,057 while other months ranged from 16 yen down to two yen up. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't look too bullish to me," the broker said. "Obviously, the economic forecast in the U.S. certainly hasn't helped people to set up positions on the buy-side." \par \par \uc2  Palladium also ended mixed. Benchmark April 2003 shed six yen per gram to 1,410 and other contracts ranged from 12 yen down to two yen up. \par \par \uc2  Silver rose across the board after a rally to a 16-month high in New York. Benchmark April 2003 gained 1.5 yen to 193.2 and other months tacked on 1.2 to 2.3 yen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020530dy5u00gd9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
