\par\par \b \uc2  Euro hits 14-month high vs dollar on recovery fears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 750 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2002\par 09:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 30 (Reuters) - The euro climbed to a 14-month high against the dollar on Thursday on continued worries that the U.S. economic recovery may be losing pace, while the yen edged back up towards levels that prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , carrying over the bullish sentiment from offshore trade, rose to 93.70 cents around midday. As of 0540 GMT, it stood at 93.62. \par \par \uc2  "I think the euro will go higher, probably to around the $0.9500 level," a trader at a major Japanese bank said. "Though the pace of gains may be slow due to profit-taking along the way, there is no reason why the euro shouldn't rise more." \par \par \uc2  Renewed speculative demand for the euro was triggered by data released on Wednesday by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that showed net capital inflows into the euro zone in March, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB's data and the recent bearishness of U.S. stocks both appear to have contributed to the euro's strength," said Junya Tanase, global markets officer at Chase Manhattan Bank. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also supported against the yen on commercial demand and gains made against the dollar, inching up to 116.26 yen against 116.18 in late New York and 115.52 in Tokyo on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Overseas speculative accounts and Japanese banks are buying euros for yen," said another trader at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  WEAKER DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The dollar also slipped against the yen, hovering around 124.21 yen against 124.38 in late U.S. trade. Traders remained cautious about selling the greenback further as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by offloading yen. \par \par \uc2  Twice last week, Japanese monetary authorities stepped in to prop up the greenback when it fell below 124 yen. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen is trapped between a bearish dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Koichi Abe, manager at Aozora Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading section. \par \par \uc2  "I think there are still some people waiting to sell dollars, so it (future movements) would pretty much depend on how much the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would absorb such demand." \par \par \uc2  The market has been worried about the speed of the U.S. recovery, especially after recent losses on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average closed down 0.59 percent at 9,923.04 on Wednesday, while the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite Index shed 1.68 percent to 1,624.39. \par \par \uc2  But some traders said they expected the dollar's weakness to be short-lived. \par \par \uc2  "Comparing the economies of the United States, Europe and Japan, I think the U.S. economy remains the strongest," said Aozora's Abe. \par \par \uc2  "The recent dollar-selling trend is due to disappointment over the recovery speed of the U.S. economy, so the trend could change in the second quarter." \par \par \uc2  Though most players expect Moody's Investors Service to cut Japan's sovereign debt rating by one notch, many think that would unlikely radically change the dollar's downward trend since the market is now focusing on the U.S. economy and stocks. \par \par \uc2  "A downgrade has been fully factored in. We just want to confirm it," said the first bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Expectations of an imminent two-notch cut have receded after Moody's upgraded Italy this month, but such a move is still seen possible. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0541 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   29 May. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9360   0.9343   +0.18    +5.12   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        124.21   124.38   -0.14    -5.64   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    116.27   116.18   +0.08    -0.83   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.5681   1.5670   +0.07    -5.50   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr        1.4620   1.4624   -0.03    +0.54   1.4541\par  Can/dlr        1.5346   1.5344   +0.01    -3.59   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr        0.5643   0.5626   +0.30   +10.52   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4685   1.4671   +0.10    -0.64   1.4780\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/stg    0.6401   0.6393   +0.13    +4.54   0.6123\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ..  Debt... Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Official rates...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......   Top events........\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Diaries........... Diaries Index........\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press Digests....  Polls on G7 economies..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Reuters Television.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba000