\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 1-Asian shares slide, as does U.S. dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Bill Tarrant \par \uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2002\par 09:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 30 (Reuters) - Asian shares slid on Thursday following Wall Street's stumbling lead, while the euro climbed to a 14-month high against the dollar in Asian trade on continued worries the U.S. economic recovery may be losing pace. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking pushed spot gold back to $324.60 an ounce in Asia after it soared to a five-year high of $327.05 in London on Wednesday, when investors sought a safe haven amid fears of an India-Pakistan war and a slumping dollar. \par \par \uc2  Asian tech shares fell over worries America's fledgling economic recovery will be too weak to invigorate corporate profits and boost spending on computer products - a key export for many Asian economies. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks closed lower as the market awaited an anticipated rating move on Japan and nervously eyed tensions in South Asia and the Middle East. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei average closed 0.70 percent lower at 11,770.03, its third straight day of losses. U.S. credit rating agency Moody's Investors Service has said it would make a decision on Japan's sovereign debt rating on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "We're now surrendering to uncertainty - Moody's decision on Japan's rating, the situation in the Middle East and a possible India-Pakistan war," said Masaharu Sakudo, managing director at Tachibana Securities. "They're all working against us." \par \par \uc2  The euro was hovering around 93.60 cents at 0630 GMT, stimulated by a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  report on Wednesday that showed net capital inflows into the euro zone in March. \par \par \uc2  "I think the euro will go higher, probably to around the $0.9500 level," a trader at a major Japanese banks said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also slipped against the yen, hovering around the 124.00 level against 124.38 in late U.S. trade, with traders expecting Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the greenback if it falls much further. It did so twice last week. \par \par \uc2  STRONGER \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2 , WEAKER U.S. \par \par \uc2  Disappointments from energy trader El Paso Corp and chip production equipment maker Novellus Systems Inc helped drive U.S. stocks lower, albeit in thin volume. The Dow Jones industrial average fell 0.59 percent to 9,923.04, while the tech-stacked Nasdaq lost 1.68 percent to 1,624.39. \par \par \uc2  Novellus spooked the market by boosting expectations for second quarter orders but declining to give a financial forecast for the second half. \par \par \uc2  South Korean stocks fell more than two percent as weakness in the U.S. markets, coupled with a stronger won, dragged down exporters and technology firms. \par \par \uc2  The Korea Composite Stock Price Index lost 2.34 percent to close at 815.61 \par \par \uc2  But Taiwan shares climbed two percent with financial stocks leading the charge, as banks accelerate bad debt write-offs to improve their financial structure. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan Weighted Index closed up 2.01 percent at 5,736.19, with the banking and insurance sub-index surging 4.4 percent. The electronics subindex rose just 0.74 percent. \par \par \uc2  Australian shares closed firmer with resource stocks, such as Rio Tinto and BHP Billiton , providing the muscle on the back of the recent gold rally. The benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index gained 0.17 percent to close at 3,387.1. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong shares were at their lowest levels in a month over nagging worries about economic recovery in the United States, the territory's second-largest trade partner. Just before 0730 GMT, the Hang Seng Index had shed 0.42 percent to 11,382.58. \par \par \uc2  Singapore shares were staging a modest comeback in the afternoon after falling as much as one percent early, as the city state joined in the general malaise over world tensions and the uncertain U.S. recovery. The Straits Times Index was flat. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020530dy5u00ez3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
