\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar struggles to stave off new lows vs euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2002\par 10:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 29 (Reuters) - The dollar struggled to stave off fresh downward pressure from the euro on Wednesday, hurt by concerns over the pace of U.S. recovery and a poor day on Wall Street, while \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears kept it afloat versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Ministry officials repeated standard warnings Japan could sell yen for dollars to curb the yen's export-damaging strength, with Vice Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda saying the MOF would act in the market when necessary. \par \par \uc2  The dollar got some relief in early Europe when the Swiss National Bank nudged its repo rate lower, helping the greenback off the day's lows against the Swiss franc and lifting it away from Tuesday's eight-month low beyond $0.93 per euro. \par \par \uc2  "Generally underperformance of U.S. asset markets is still contraining the dollar and the euro is benefiting as a result," said Russell Jones, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  By 0800 GMT the dollar was trading at $0.9285 per euro, barely change from late New York levels but uncomfortably close to its trough last September at $0.9333. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the greenback was hovering around 124.55 yen. It was confined to a 30-tick range but only one yen above a 5-1/2 month low that brought Japanese authorities into the market selling yen for dollars two days in a row last week. \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE THREATS \par \par \uc2  Stern reminders from Japanese officials did little to galvanise the market, where the consensus is for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar falls below 124.00 yen. \par \par \uc2  Reaction to mixed Japanese industrial data was also muted. Output at Japanese factories rose 0.2 percent in April from March, weaker than the 1.0 percent gain analysts had expected. \par \par \uc2  But it was the third straight month of rise after many months of decline, and a sharp drop in inventories was also interpreted as encouraging since it could set the stage for further increases. \par \par \uc2  "The data confirmed the manufacturing sector has entered a recovery phase," said Takehiro Sato, economist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  But the figures did nothing to change the current dollar-bearish bias, since market players still view U.S. asset markets as relatively unappealing for now. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average broke below 10,000 on Tuesday, with dealers saying a fall below last month's low around 9,800 could worsen sentiment for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Exacerbating the dollar's misfortune, Japanese stocks have outperformed U.S. equities by nearly 25 percent on a dollar basis so far this year, making it difficult for global investors not to have Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  "The market is watching to see if the Dow slips below 9,800. It's also important how the Nikkei reacts. Will it fall in tandem, or show resilience?" a dealer at a Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  The market also has an eye on Moody's Investors Service, which has said a decision on its rating review on Japan is due by the end of this month. \par \par \uc2  The market is generally expecting a one-notch downgrade, which would bring Japan to A1 from the current Aa3. \par \par \uc2  EURO BENEFITS \par \par \uc2  The euro had a slightly softer tone against the yen, holding just below late New York levels at 115.57 yen but set a 14-month high against sterling. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has done a bit better on the crosses over the last 24-36 hours so the technical background is still looking pretty positive," Jones at Lehman said, adding this was more catch-up from a lagging position than for fundamentally euro-bullish reasons. \par \par \uc2  However comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  council member and Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke late on Tuesday were cited as euro-supportive. \par \par \uc2  Welteke said a recent pay rise won by German engineering union IG Metall was slightly higher than the ECB had expected, but that he was not worried about its economic impact. \par \par \uc2  Some ECB members have voiced fears over whether wage settlements recently struck in Germany were compatible with the ECB's price stability goals. The next policy setting meeting is scheduled for next week and while many in the market are not expecting a rate hike until the third quarter, the ECB's shift to a more hawkish tone recently has created some uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  Switzerland pared its repo rate two basis points to 1.01 percent in its ongoing attempt to combat Swiss franc strength which it fears could potentially damage economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The move helped the dollar recover to 1.5750 Swiss francs from session lows below 1.5720. The Swiss franc also fell to retest one-month lows near 1.4640 per euro. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020529dy5t00go3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
