\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar flat - struggles to fight off pessimism. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2002\par 09:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 29 (Reuters) - The dollar failed to fight off lingering doubts over an economic recovery in the United States, staying under pressure in Asia on Wednesday although propped up by the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar held near its late New York level of 124.41/49 yen throughout the day. At 0545 GMT, it was quoted at 124.54/56. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Ministry officials repeated their standard remarks on Wednesday, with Vice Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda saying in Jakarta that the ministry will act in the market when necessary. \par \par \uc2  The boiler plate comment did little to inspire the market, where the consensus is for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it did twice last week, if the dollar falls below 124.00 yen. \par \par \uc2  Reaction was also muted to mixed Japanese industrial output data announced in the morning. \par \par \uc2  Output at Japan's factories rose 0.2 percent in April from March, weaker than the 1.0 percent gain expected by 18 economists polled by Reuters. \par \par \uc2  But it was the third straight month of rise after many months of decline, and a sharp drop in inventories was also interpreted as encouraging since it could set the stage for further increases. \par \par \uc2  "The data confirmed the manufacturing sector has entered a recovery phase," said Takehiro Sato, economist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  At the end of the day, though, the figures did nothing to change the current dollar-bearish bias, since market players still view the U.S. asset market as relatively unappealing for the time being. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average broke below 10,000 on Tuesday, with dealers saying a fall below last month's low around 9,800 could worsen sentiment for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Exacerbating the dollar's luck, Japanese stocks have outperformed U.S. equities by nearly 25 percent on a dollar basis so far this year, making it difficult for global investors not to have Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  "The market is watching to see if the Dow slips below 9,800. It's also important how the Nikkei reacts. Will it fall in tandem, or show resilience?" a dealer at a Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  The market also has an eye on Moody's Investors Service, which has said it a decision on its rating review on Japan was due by the end of this month. \par \par \uc2  The market is generally expecting a one-notch downgrade, which would bring Japan to A1 from the current Aa3. \par \par \uc2  EURO EYES NEW HIGH \par \par \uc2  The euro also held firm around 92.85 cents, little changed from its late New York level and off an eight-month peak of 93.07 hit the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, it was flat at 115.68/74 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The euro's bullishness is simply a reflection of a weak tone on the dollar. There are few fundamental factors that are particularly bullish for the euro at the moment," said Junya Tanase, Global Markets Officer at Chase Manhattan Bank. \par \par \uc2  Traders see the next target at 93.35, a high set in the aftermath of the attacks in the United States last September. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar has been too strong for the past three years and the market is in adjustment. If the current momentum lasts, the euro will test the 96-to 98-cent area," said Kazunari Kobayashi, Treasury Manager at State Street in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Tanase of Chase also said: "If the euro clears that hurdle, people would be more comfortable believing that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's major bottom was 82.28 (set in October 2000) and that the euro will challenge dollar parity sooner rather than later." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0551 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   28 May. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9284   0.9277   +0.08    +4.27   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        124.61   124.54   +0.06    -5.33   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    115.65   115.62   +0.03    -1.36   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.5737   1.5736   +0.01    -5.16   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr        1.4583   1.4586   -0.02    +0.29   1.4541\par  Can/dlr        1.5318   1.5305   +0.08    -3.76   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr        0.5617   0.5611   +0.11   +10.01   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4615   1.4612   +0.02    -1.12   1.4780\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/stg    0.6366   0.6358   +0.13    +3.97   0.6123\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ..  Debt... Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Official rates...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......   Top events........\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Diaries........... Diaries Index........\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 