\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar stuck,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears counter exporters. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2002\par 08:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 28 (Reuters) - The dollar moved little in Asia on Tuesday, trapped between caution over possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) and yen bids from Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials, worried that a strong yen would hurt its export-led economic recovery, kept up their verbal threats on Tuesday after the BOJ stepped in twice last week to sell the yen as the dollar fell below 124 yen. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said he would monitor fluctuations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with vigilance. \par \par \uc2  "The movement from yen weakness to yen strength was too drastic. I don't think the current market conditions will stabilise," he told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  In what market participants guessed was an attempt to discourage yen buying, Shiokawa also said he was not optimistic about Japan's January-March gross domestic product (GDP), which analysts expect would show crisp growth. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of 26 economists released on Tuesday produced a median forecast of 6.2 percent annualised growth, which would beat the U.S. growth rate of 5.6 percent for the same quarter. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar made little headway, with many Japanese exporters said to be lined up with sell orders above 125 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hovered around 124.70 yen throughout Asian trade, compared with 124.69 late in holiday-thinned U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  "The floor (for the dollar) is pretty solid due to the BOJ," said Takashi Toyahara, a manager at Nomura Securities. "There aren't any incentives to buy the dollar, so we're basically stuck in this area." \par \par \uc2  The euro was also subdued, at $0.9213 against $0.9206 in late New York, after having failed to capitalise on a positive surprise in German Ifo survey of business sentiment for May on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Ifo sentiment index came in at 91.5, above the average forecast of 90.6 in a Reuters poll and up from 90.5 in April. \par \par \uc2  "The figure was good but the euro didn't move. Too many people were already long on the euro," Toyahara said. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was little changed at 114.81 yen. \par \par \uc2  EYES ON U.S. DATA \par \par \uc2  In the absence of other market-moving factors, the market will be watching U.S. consumer confidence data for May, due at 1400 GMT, for clues on the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that if the numbers are weak, that could give bears an excuse to sell off the dollar. If the figure is stronger than expected, though, it still wouldn't help the dollar enough to reverse the bearish sentiment, they said. \par \par \uc2  Japan will announce industrial output for April on Wednesday morning. \par \par \uc2  Mounting tension in South Asia has so far had a limited impact on the market, with participants as yet unsure as to how to interpret the latest events. \par \par \uc2  Pakistan on Tuesday conducted its third missile test amid growing tension with neighbour India. President Pervez Musharraf said on Monday Pakistan would not start a war, but would respond "with full might" if attacked. \par \par \uc2  Indian Defence Minister George Fernandes said on Tuesday New Delhi saw no signs of reason from Pakistan and that India's options are narrowing. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been unable to react to the recent ominous developments in South Asia," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "No one knows where this is leading to." \par