\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  options-Barrier options help cap euro gains. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kyle Peterson \par \uc2 442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2002\par 20:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, May 28 (Reuters) - A series of euro options defenses at 93 cents and above curtailed intraday gains on Tuesday for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which climbed to its highest level versus the dollar since September. \par \par \uc2  The euro ran into a tangle of barrier options on its way to 93.09 cents. Barrier options give the holder a right to trade an amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a set price for a specified time. But such options become worthless when the market hits an agreed-upon price. \par \par \uc2  Often, buyers of barrier options will buy or sell \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the strike price in hopes of keeping their option alive. Traders noted strikes for barriers up to 93.30 cents. \par \par \uc2  "Apparently there's 93 (cent) area barriers," said Ken Boyer, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS. "There might still be some barriers out there that were placed just above 93 (cents). Some were trying to protect it earlier. There's sort of a tug-of-war here." \par \par \uc2  The euro moved higher in earlier U.S. dealings amid a decline in U.S. stocks and disappointing U.S. economic data. The euro rally also trigged a move up in implied volatility for front-end euro options to 8.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  Volatility, which is a measure of how much the options market expects the price of the underlying asset to move during the life of the option, was near 8.8 percent, one New York options trader said. \par \par \uc2  "That's up a little bit from this morning," he said. \par \par \uc2  The 25-delta one-month risk reversal was 0.6/0.8 favoring euro calls. Risk reversals measure the market's preference for calls versus puts. \par \par \uc2  Volatility for one-month yen options was 8.25 percent after the dollar trimmed losses from a three-session low of 124.29 yen. The dollar remained in the May 23 range as dealers contemplated the likelihood of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ bought dollars and sold yen on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday and Thursday in an effort to weaken the yen. Officials have complained that yen strength was a hindrance to the Japanese export market and therefore to Japan's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Boyer said that volatility for yen options probably would increase if the dollar renewed its losses against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "I kind of expect that if it sells off below 124, vols would go up again," he said. \par \par \uc2  The 25-delta one-month risk reversal was 0.6/1.0 percent favoring yen calls. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020528dy5s0104b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
