\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar eyes eight-month lows against euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2002\par 12:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 28 (Reuters) - The dollar slid back within sight of eight-month lows against the euro as concerns over the strength of the U.S. recovery resurfaced ahead of U.S. consumer confidence data due later on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But the yen's rise against the greenback was capped by the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is concerned that the strength of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which fell to 5-1/2 month lows last week, will derail its fragile export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  "There's growing confidence in the view that the dollar has turned. But there is limited scope for yen appreciation because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at BankOne. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 124.60 yen at 0946 GMT, slightly weaker on the day but up almost a yen from levels at which the Bank of Japan bought dollars on Wednesday and Thursday of last week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was down more than half a percent at $0.9260 per euro, moving back within sight of last week's eight-month low of $0.9280. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen over six percent against the yen and five percent against the euro since the beginning of April as concern over the sustainability of the U.S. recovery has dimmed the allure of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  "The market is showing no signs that it has finished with taking the dollar down," said Russell Jones, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  JITTERS \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa kept threats of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alive, saying he would monitor fluctuations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with vigilance. \par \par \uc2  "The movement from yen weakness to yen strength was too drastic. I don't think the current market conditions will stabilise," he told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  In what market participants guessed was an attempt to discourage yen buying, Shiokawa also played down expectations of a big rise in first quarter gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  Analysts polled by Reuters on Tuesday expected the Japanese economy to expand by an annualised pace of 6.2 percent in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi also reiterated that stable moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates were desirable and that the ministry was continuing to monitor the market. \par \par \uc2  "Second-guessing on whether and when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made the market reluctant to sell dollars, but there is no good reason to buy," said Nick Parsons, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  EYES ON U.S. DATA \par \par \uc2  In the absence of other market-moving factors, the market will be watching U.S. consumer confidence data for May, due at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that if the numbers are weak, that could give bears an excuse to sell off the dollar. If the figure is stronger than expected, though, it still would not help the dollar enough to reverse the bearish sentiment, they said. \par \par \uc2  Japan will announce industrial output for April on Wednesday morning. \par \par \uc2  Mounting tension in South Asia has so far had a limited impact on the market, with participants as yet unsure as to how to interpret the latest events. \par \par \uc2  Pakistan on Tuesday conducted its third missile test in four days amid growing tension with neighbour India. President Pervez Musharraf said on Monday Pakistan would not start a war, but would respond "with full might" if attacked. \par \par \uc2  Indian Defence Minister George Fernandes said on Tuesday New Delhi saw no signs of reason from Pakistan and that India's options are narrowing. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020528dy5s00k2l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
