\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar nudges up as euro sheds Ifo gains. \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Thornhill \par \uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2002\par 14:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 27 (Reuters) - The dollar traded mildly firmer against the euro and the yen in holiday-thinned trading on Monday with a lack of fresh market impetus giving the greenback some respite after last week's losses. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit clawed back lost ground against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after dipping to lows around $0.9220 earlier on after a surprisingly strong German Ifo survey of business sentiment for May, but a national holiday in the U.S. made for subdued trade. \par \par \uc2  "Euro/dollar remains a dollar story, rather than a euro one, and we really need to see significantly worse U.S. data to push the dollar further down," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at WestLB. \par \par \uc2  A relatively light data week in the U.S. this week could make for a steadier showing for the dollar in the near-term, he added. \par \par \uc2  By 1150 GMT, the dollar was a touch up from Friday's U.S. closing levels against the euro, trading at $0.9193. The greenback hit an eight-month low of $0.9280 last week. \par \par \uc2  The yen was slightly softer against the dollar as the positive impact from strong Japanese stocks was nullified by wariness over possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  The yen traded at 124.92 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  IFO DOUBTS \par \par \uc2  The headline Ifo West German sentiment index came in at 91.5, above a consensus forecast of 90.6 and up from 90.5 in April, but the euro failed to build on an initial move higher and some analysts said the strength of the survey was somewhat confusing. \par \par \uc2  "There have to be some doubts over the Ifo number...It does not coincide with the ZEW report, and why should manufacturers have such high expectations when they have been complaining about the latest wage round," said Klawitter at WestLB. \par \par \uc2  Germany's ZEW institute's May business expectations indicator - usually seen as a good pointer to the Ifo report that follows it - fell to 66.3, from 70.6 in April, showing a drop in optimism among analysts and investors. \par \par \uc2  The economist in charge of the Ifo institute survey, Gernot Nerb, said the latest release showed that the German recovery is now in place, but added that the economy remained subdued with consumer demand still weak because of high unemployment. \par \par \uc2  Nerb also advised against a rate hike from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying the economy is still too weak and that there are no big inflation risks. \par \par \uc2  The upbeat Ifo survey did help the euro hit its highest levels in a month against the Swiss franc, briefly touching 1.46. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on, there was positive news on the German inflation front with April producer prices easing 0.1 percent on the month, down 0.8 percent on the year - their biggest annual drop since mid-1999. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  NERVES DOG YEN \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Nikkei average reached 12,081.43 on Monday, its highest since August 8, on hopes that a recovering economy would help banks write off huge bad loans. \par \par \uc2  But the yen could not capitalise, with dealers worried about the potential for further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit its strength. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities, nervous that a strong yen could hurt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-led recovery, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday and Thursday to buy the greenback after the yen firmed to a 5-1/2 month high of 123.50 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  "For the yen, wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still so strong and the dollar is holding onto gains made last week even with the gains in Nikkei," said David Mann, treasury economist at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese authorities on Monday kept up their pressure against a strengthening yen, with senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi saying he was still monitoring the market. \par \par \uc2  Focus is now shifting on to the release of Japan's gross domestic product figures for the Japan-March quarter on June 7, which are widely expected to show growth of perhaps as much as eight or nine percent on an annualised basis. \par \par \uc2  "If the (Japanese GDP) figures are as good as many predict, another sell-yen factor will leave the market," said Kazuo Takayama, vice president at Toronto-Dominion Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Ratings firm Moody's Investors Service is expected to announce some time this week the decision of its credit review of Japanese sovereign debt that it has been conducting since mid-February. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was little changed at 114.85. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020527dy5r00ngi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
