\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  wins  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  battle, faces longer war. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2002\par 14:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 24 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to halt an export damaging rise in the yen this week, but analysts say its dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks increasingly at odds with a recovering Japanese economy and U.S. concern about an overvalued dollar. \par \par \uc2  With U.S. exporters grumbling about an uncompetitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Tokyo is likely to have to live with a yen stronger than its recent 130-135 range and may have to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the weeks ahead to stave off yen bulls. \par \par \uc2  "(\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I think its intention is not to keep (the dollar) around 125 yen, but to completely defend 120 yen. That's why it has been stepping in," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  With Japan's hands effectively tied on fiscal and monetary policy, a weaker yen is one of the few tools left at its disposal to protect a tentative export-led rebound from its worst post-war slump. \par \par \uc2  After a six percent fall in dollar/yen since April, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars against the yen this week for the first time since late September. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was initially seen to have bought dollars around 123.80-123.90 yen on Wednesday after the greenback dropped to a five-and-a-half-month low of 123.50 on the day. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars again on Thursday below 124 yen and repeatedly above that level in European time. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood around 125 yen in late Friday trade. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the BOJ will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the same way as in September because there is a question over whether the United States would allow dollar to strengthen above 130 yen by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Hanao said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in late September after the yen rose as high as 115.87 yen per dollar in the aftermath of September 11 attacks on the United States. Heavy and repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  forced the yen below 120 by early October. \par \par \uc2  U.S. DEFICIT CONCERN \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. administration is unlikely to favour a strong dollar ahead of elections in November given that U.S. manufacturers' repeated complaints about an overvalued dollar. \par \par \uc2  A strong dollar makes U.S.-made products more expensive on global markets, and is particularly harsh on smaller firms that cannot move production lines abroad to hedge against it. \par \par \uc2  The latest complaint about the dollar's strength came from the U.S. National Association of Manufacturers (NAM) and auto giant General Motors Corp (GM) on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Jiji news agency quoted NAM President Jerry Jasinowski saying in Washington that the dollar is 25-35 percent overvalued and thus hurting U.S. exporters. \par \par \uc2  Separately, it reported GM Chief Economist Mustafa Mohatarem said that the U.S. current account now is too large and the only way to correct it is for the dollar to weaken. \par \par \uc2  The massive U.S. current account deficit is adding to pressure for a weaker dollar. Last year, the deficit contracted for the first time in seven years to $417.4 billion, but it is still the largest of any industrialised nation. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, a weak yen has helped Japan's current account surplus increase. In March, the surplus rose 51.8 percent to 2.2 trillion yen ($17.60 billion), with the trade surplus gaining 27.5 percent to 1.41 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMY BRIGHTER \par \par \uc2  Reflecting U.S. concerns, Japanese officials have stressed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not aimed at checking the yen's rise but at slowing rapid moves. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the BOJ wanted to stabilise the dollar around 125 yen, but would be reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to guide it back to the 130-135 yen range. \par \par \uc2  Japan may even face difficulty capping the yen strength, with the economy improving and domestic share prices edging higher. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is somewhat contradicting itself when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rally, which is partly due to a brighter fundamentals," a senior Japanese trust bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "We all remember that Japanese authorities have repeatedly said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect economic fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  The government and the BOJ have been steadily upgrading upgraded their economic outlooks in recent months and analysts forecast a surge in Japan's first-quarter gross domestic product growth by as much as an annualised eight or nine percent. \par \par \uc2  ($1=125.00 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020524dy5o00o3r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
