\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar bears retreat, nervous of Japan action. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2002\par 14:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 24 (Reuters) - The dollar kept a firm footing above 125 yen on Friday as dealers remained nervous that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a third day. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities bought dollars below 124 yen on Wednesday and Thursday out of concern that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent surge could derail the country's nascent export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  Speculation of an upward revision to U.S. first-quarter growth data at 1230 GMT also supported the dollar, particularly after strong durable goods data in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  "Stability is the main theme today," said Julian Jessop, economist at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar got some momentum from yesterday's strong U.S. data and firmer stocks and this, along with the threat of further Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is encouraged dealers to square positions." \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 125.15 yen at 1200 GMT, holding gains of over a yen made after Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The yen has leapt over six percent against the dollar since the start of April as concerns over the strength of the U.S. recovery have coincided with optimism that Japan's economy may be turning the corner. \par \par \uc2  Signs of easing tension between India and Pakistan also supported the dollar, which gained over half a percent to 1.5875 Swiss francs and a third of a percent to $0.9175 per euro. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit 28-month lows against the Swiss franc and eight-month lows against the euro earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN ALERT \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials made clear on Friday that they were ready to step in again if the yen rose too quickly. \par \par \uc2  "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is unchanged," Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa told a news conference. "We want \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to be stable." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, which has the largest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world, is estimated to have bought about $3 billion in Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market is not in the mood to take on the Japanese authorities today," said Steven Pearson, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Halifax. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing better dollar sentiment today and it's pushing up against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well." \par \par \uc2  Dealers added that the dollar was also benefiting from short-covering ahead of a long Memorial Day weekend in the United States. \par \par \uc2  "People are squaring up ahead of the weekend," said a trader at a Japanese bank in London. "U.S. data yesterday was better than expected and there's always a chance that U.S. GDP could be revised higher. \par \par \uc2  GOOD DATA FAILS TO LIFT EURO \par \par \uc2  Data showing strong Italian retail sales, solid French first-quarter growth and benign German inflation failed to lift the euro, as position-squaring pushed the dollar higher across the board. \par \par \uc2  Italian retail sales grew 3.0 percent year-on-year in March, one of its biggest rises in the last two years, while the French economy grew by a respectable 0.4 percent in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the German Finance Ministry said that consumer prices rose by a preliminary 1.2 percent year-on-year in May, slower than some in the market had feared. Soft inflation data from German states also helped push back expectations of when the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said sentiment toward the dollar, which has been under pressure recently from concerns about the U.S. economy, was slightly better following news of a stronger than expected 1.1 percent rise in durable goods orders released on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The durables figures are helping to ease some of the very bearish sentiment on the dollar we had at the start of the week," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020524dy5o00o3a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
