\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar wins reprieve as Japan threats heeded. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2002\par 12:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 24 (Reuters) - The dollar kept a firm footing above 125 yen on Friday as dealers remained nervous that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a third straight day. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities bought dollars below 124 yen on Wednesday and Thursday, concerned that the recent surge in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could derail the country's nascent export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  "So far they have been successful in protecting 124 though we still think it will be taken out. It's just the start of the battle," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at the Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "If it looks like we're taking out 124, then we'll probably see them in again." \par \par \uc2  The yen has leapt over six percent against the dollar since the start of April as concerns over the strength of the U.S. recovery have coincided with optimism that Japan's economy may be turning the corner. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 125.20 yen in the European midsession, up almost two yen from five-month lows set on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The greenback also gained against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leaping 0.6 percent against the Swiss franc to 1.5875 francs as signs of easing tension between India and Pakistan encouraged an unwinding of safe-haven trades. \par \par \uc2  Gains against the Swiss franc helped lift the dollar a third of a percent to $0.9175 per euro, recovering more than a cent from eight-month lows hit on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  