\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar steady as market wary of BoJ. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2002\par 10:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 24 (Reuters) - The dollar was locked in a tight range around 125 yen on Friday as dealers remained nervous that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, selling its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the third day running. \par \par \uc2  Japan bought dollars below 124 yen on Wednesday and Thursday, concerned that the strength of the yen, which rose to 5-1/2 month highs around 123.50 this week, could derail Japan's tentative export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  "So far they have been successful in protecting 124 though we still think it will be taken out. It's just the start of the battle," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at the Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "If it looks like we're taking out 124, then we'll probably see them in again." \par \par \uc2  At 0733 GMT, the dollar was trading at 124.92 yen, unchanged on the day and around a yen higher than the level at which the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also steady against the dollar, trading just around $0.92, compared with eight-month highs of $0.9280 hit on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN READY \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have made it clear they will step into the market again if the yen rises too quickly. \par \par \uc2  "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is unchanged," Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa told a news conference on Friday. "We want \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to be stable." \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimate the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought about $3 billion in Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The market was slow ahead of the long Memorial Day weekend in the United States. There will be limited U.S. trading on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "You can't sell the dollar because of concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Takashi Toyahara, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "On the other hand, Japanese exporters are ready to sell above 125 yen." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said sentiment toward the dollar, which has been under pressure recently from concerns about the U.S. economy, was slightly better following news of a stronger than expected 1.1 percent rise in durable goods orders released on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The durables figures are helping to ease some of the very bearish sentiment on the dollar we had at the start of the week," said Hawkins. \par \par \uc2  Revised U.S. gross domestic product data for the first quarter will be released at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that an apparent easing of tensions between India and Pakistan was also helping the dollar maintain a firmer footing. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was up a third of a percent against the Swiss franc, trading at 1.5833 francs. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, news that the French economy grew by 0.4 percent in the first quarter had little impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But analysts said that soft inflation data out of the German states was helping to push back expectations of when the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise interest rates. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020524dy5o00fxi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
