\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-BOJ hints  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  unsterilised. \b0\par\par\uc2 375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2002\par 04:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 24 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) said on Friday it added liquidity to the money market with funds from recent yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suggesting the move was unsterilised. \par \par \uc2  "We are providing ample liquidity to the market including the money generated through \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a BOJ official said. "It is under the directive of our money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the 10-15 trillion yen current account target." \par \par \uc2  Unsterilised yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  means the BOJ leaves the extra yen funds in the money market, usually a sign the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is loosening credit. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the move had little meaning under the BOJ's current so-called quantitative easing policy, under which it keeps the money market awash with funds and holds short-term interest rates virtually at zero. \par \par \uc2  "It just means the BOJ will hold back its usual money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make way for the funds from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It doesn't change its reserves target," said Yasushi Okada, chief economist at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  A Japanese bank trader also said the unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't signify further easing because the money provided to the banking system through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  represented only a small part of the liquidity. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the current account balance that commercial banks, brokers and financial institutions hold at the BOJ was estimated to be slightly above target at 15.2 trillion yen, unchanged from Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The money injected into the banking system through yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  represents a part of that amount, bankers said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers reckoned several billion dollars were bought from banks in Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meaning banks in turn received several hundred billion yen worth of funds as a result of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bankers said. \par \par \uc2  Those deals would be settled on Friday, given the two-day settlement period for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ currently leaves about 15 trillion yen worth of credit in the banking system each day through money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as purchases of treasury and commercial bills. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold yen for the second day in a row on Thursday to prevent a tentative export-led economic recovery from being derailed by a strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020524dy5o003jt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
