\par\par \b \uc2  COMEX gold stalls early but world tensions limit losses. \b0\par\par\uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 16:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 23 (Reuters) - COMEX gold's week-long ascent stalled Thursday morning on profit-taking, although the safe-haven asset held at slightly lower levels amid jitters about simmering Israeli-Palestinian violence, conflict in disputed Kashmir and wobbly U.S. stocks, floor traders said. \par \par \uc2  New York's June gold futures contract had backed off from Wednesday's 21-month high near $319 an ounce but managed to tread water above downside support pegged near $315. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said earlier remarks from Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman Sergei Ignatyev about possible gold reserve sales got the market's attention, sparking some confusion among traders. \par \par \uc2  "We were really focusing on the Russian headlines. Some speculators were shorting the market this morning with the Russian news. It doesn't look like there's been any real liquidation though this morning," one COMEX broker said. \par \par \uc2  Ignatyev said he did not mean to say that a projected sell-off of its reserves would include gold. "I did not mean gold reserves, I meant hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," Ignatyev told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Benchmark June gold at 0954 EDT was down $1.50 at $316.80 an ounce, trading between $315.60 and $318.50. Estimated volume at 10:00 a.m. was 15,000 contracts. \par \par \uc2  "I think we have to get above $325 in June gold to break out on the upside, and it should hold (down) at the $315.50 level," said another COMEX floor trader. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold fetched $316.90/7.40, down from Wednesday's New York close at $318.10/60. Bullion in London fixed at $316.25 this morning, off a spot high at $319.60 previously - its strongest since October 1999. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said gold was still supported by turbulence in Israel and at the Pakistani-Indian border in Jammu and Kashmir, and by a generally weak U.S. dollar which has encouraged overseas bullion buying. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, suspected Palestinian militants tried to set ablaze a major Israeli fuel pumping depot near Tel Aviv by detonating a bomb on it, but workers at the plant outside Herliza extinguished a fire the blast caused before it spread. That came hours after a suicide bomber struck in another Israeli town. \par \par \uc2  Pakistan on Thursday put its capital on a war footing, while India softened its rhetoric a day after its prime minister told troops in Kashmir to prepare for a decisive fight against separatists. Both armies this week exchanged heavy fire across the mountainous border. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar got a boost against the euro from unexpectedly strong U.S. April durable goods data. The greenback also rose against the yen after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  U.S. equities traded a shade lower during the morning. \par \par \uc2  July silver drooped 5.0 cents to $4.79 an ounce, scaling back from its recent surge, traded $4.845 to $4.77. Spot silver was quoted at $4.77/79 versus its prior close at $4.82/84. It fixed in London at $477.50 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  NYMEX July platinum was down $4 at $536 an ounce. Spot platinum was indicated at $538/545. \par \par \uc2  Illiquid June palladium shed $7 to $354 an ounce. Spot palladium was at $348/363. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020523dy5n00rhn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
