\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar leaps after Japan sells yen for 2nd day. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 14:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 23 (Reuters) - The dollar jumped nearly a yen within seconds on Thursday after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for the second day running, trying to curb the export-damaging strength of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it had taken "appropriate action" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and said that it was continuously acting in the market. \par \par \uc2  "We reckon the Japanese were in at 123.90 and then bang, bang, here we go again. It just went flying," said Neil Parker, market strategist at RBS Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  The greenback leapt from just around 123.90 yen to a high above 124.90 by 1204 GMT. The euro also rose against the yen to 115.22 from 114.95 late in New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Japan, worried that the yen's rise could derail the country's export-led recovery, sold its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday for the first time since September, pushing the dollar to 125.08 yen after it had touched 5-1/2 month lows around 123.50. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of England declined to comment on Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ECB has in the past conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in European trading hours. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated the Bank of Japan, acting on the MOF's behalf, bought around $3 billion on Wednesday and roughly the same on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  WILL IT WORK? \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to be successful in reversing the yen's rise in the longer term and further action was almost certain over the next few days. \par \par \uc2  The yen has been rising in recent weeks as the outlook for Japan's long-suffering economy improves and share prices there are seen offering better value at a time when investors are questioning the relative attractiveness of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  "The question is whether you go agressively long of dollars or whether this is just a pause in the dollar's descent," said Jesper Dannesboe, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. \par \par \uc2  "You may want to buy dollars in the short term if you're day trading but you don't want to be strategically positioned for the dollar to go up." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that while September's BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been successful in helping to trigger a 20 yen rise in the dollar over four months, there were more fundamental flows working against the dollar this time. \par \par \uc2  Japan acted in September to offset yen strength as Japanese corporations repatriated assets to shore up balance sheets ahead of fiscal half-year end. \par \par \uc2  EURO SOFT \par \par \uc2  The dollar retained a firmer footing against the euro, gaining half a cent to $0.9225 from eight-month lows hit on Wednesday as concern over the U.S. recovery and relative appeal of U.S. assets continued to weigh on the greenback. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed little reaction to news German economic growth sputtered back into life in the first three months of this year, reignited by a jump-start from exports. \par \par \uc2  Gross domestic product grew by 0.2 percent in the first quarter from the final quarter of 2001, when it contracted by 0.3 percent, the Federal Statistics Office said. \par \par \uc2  The pick-up in the first quarter, which followed two quarters of shrinking economic activity, came as little surprise after the Bundesbank this week estimated GDP rose around 0.25 percent in the first three months of this year. \par \par \uc2  The data beat analysts' forecasts for growth of 0.1 percent on the quarter. But economists warned that the foundations of the upturn were shaky, dependent largely on foreign demand rather than domestic consumer spending. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020523dy5n00ngu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
