\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Determined  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes \highlight\uc2  to sell yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 11:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 23 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold yen on Thursday for the second day in a row, underscoring its determination to prevent a tentative export-led recovery from being derailed by a surge in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar swiftly rose almost one full yen to around 124.80 yen after the Bank of Japan came into the market on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF). \par \par \uc2  "We took appropriate action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today just like yesterday," a MOF official told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Not content with a single sally into the market, the authorities kept dealers on their guard by repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , officials said. \par \par \uc2  They declined to say which \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  they bought in exchange for yen but on Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars for the first time since September 28 after the yen climbed briefly to 123.50 to the dollar, its highest level in 5-= months. \par \par \uc2  The yen has been strong in recent days because of growing evidence that Japan has pulled out of its third recession in a decade. Economists expect growth in the first quarter could have reached an annualised rate of more than eight percent, handily outpacing the United States. \par \par \uc2  Trade figures released earlier in the day showed that the recovery is being powered by exports. \par \par \uc2  Japan shipped five percent more goods in April than a year earlier in volume terms, and the value of the exports rose 1.6 percent, the first increase in 13 months. \par \par \uc2  Those gains could be threatened if the yen were to rise too far too fast because Japanese exports could be priced out of the United States and other key markets by South Korea and other Asian competitors. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Wednesday that he was unhappy with the speed of the yen's recent ascent. \par \par \uc2  "It's problematic for everyone when the yen moves rapidly, by five, seven yen in one week," he told a news conference. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020523dy5n00j52\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
