\par\par \b \uc2  Hayami says  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  was needed as moves rapid. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yonggi Kang \par \uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 11:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 23 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said on Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had acted correctly by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars against yen a day earlier since market moves had been rapid. \par \par \uc2  But he also said the yen's moves against the dollar over the past few months, when it rose strongly, reflected the market's view of the relative strength of the Japanese and U.S. economies. \par \par \uc2  "I think it was good that necessary action was taken against the rapid moves," he told a news conference, declining to comment on the effectiveness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or specific exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  Hayami pointed out that the yen is not much stronger against the dollar than it was at the beginning of last year. \par \par \uc2  "People say that the yen is too strong now, but it's about the same as the first half of last year," he said. \par \par \uc2  Hayami, known to be a proponent of a strong yen said it was difficult to manipulate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates as they were affected by an array of factors. \par \par \uc2  "If the yen weakens, that will be good for the economy and exports may rise, but that's easier said than done," he said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, acting on behalf of the Finance Ministry, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the dollar on Wednesday after it fell rapidly to a five-month low of 123.50 yen from around 128 in a few days on worries about U.S. asset markets. \par \par \uc2  The greenback hovered around 124.30 yen in Tokyo on Thursday, with traders wary of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Hayami said he saw no major discrepancy in assessments of the economy by the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite differing views on the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  The government and the BOJ both upgraded their official economic views for the third straight month in May, but economists say the government view is more bullish since it has declared a bottom has been hit. \par \par \uc2  "We do not use the phrase 'hitting bottom' at the Bank of Japan," Hayami said. "But I don't think there's a huge difference between (the assessments of) the government and the BOJ." \par \par \uc2  In its monthly report, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday the pace of deterioration in the economy had moderated, with production starting to pick up. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ left its monetary policy unchanged on Tuesday, after a series of easing steps since March 2001 that lowered interest rates to near zero and ensured virtually unlimited funds to the market from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The nine-member board, which also kept policy unchanged at its April meeting, voted unanimously to keep its monthly buying of Japanese government bonds at one trillion yen ($8.05 billion) and reiterated its pledge to offer even more liquidity in the event of a crisis. \par \par \uc2  ($=124.28 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020523dy5n00hwz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
