\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 3-Hayami says  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  was right to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yonggi Kang \par \uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 12:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 23 (Reuters) - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami said on Thursday that Japanese authorities had done the right thing by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars against the yen this week because the market was moving too quickly. \par \par \uc2  But the governor, known as a proponent of a strong yen, also said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise against the dollar over the past few months reflected the market's view of the relative strength of the Japanese and U.S. economies. \par \par \uc2  MOF officials, apparently worried that the fast-rising yen was a danger to Japan's nascent economic recovery, conducted an active campaign to try to talk it down before the BOJ stepped into the market on the ministry's behalf on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  And just hours after Hayami spoke, the BOJ stepped in once again during London trading hours to cap the yen's rise. The dollar rose almost one full yen to around 124.80 yen on the latest yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The comments by Hayami, who often does not see eye-to-eye with the government on policy, underline the sensitive position he is in. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has no say in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and it only acts in the market as an agent for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I think it was good that necessary action was taken against the rapid moves," he told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  He would not comment on the effectiveness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or on specific rates, but he noted the yen was not much stronger against the dollar now than it was at the start of last year. \par \par \uc2  "People say that the yen is too strong now, but it's about the same as the first half of last year," he said. \par \par \uc2  Hayami said it was difficult to manipulate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates as they were affected by an array of factors. \par \par \uc2  "If the yen weakens, that will be good for the economy and exports may rise, but that's easier said than done," he said. \par \par \uc2  "I basically think the yen is not such a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  NO GAP WITH GOVERNMENT \par \par \uc2  Hayami also said he saw no major discrepancy between the BOJ and the government in their assessments of the economy, despite differing views on the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  The government and the BOJ both upgraded their official economic assessments for the third straight month in May, but economists say the government view is more bullish since it declared that the economy had hit bottom. \par \par \uc2  "We do not use the phrase 'hitting bottom' at the Bank of Japan," Hayami said. "But I don't think there's a huge difference between (the assessments of) the government and the BOJ." \par \par \uc2  In its monthly report released on Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the pace of deterioration in the economy had moderated and that production had started to pick up. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ left its monetary policy unchanged after its two-day policy board meeting ended on Tuesday after a series of easing steps since March 2001 that lowered interest rates to near zero. \par \par \uc2  The nine-member board, which also kept policy unchanged at its April meeting, voted unanimously to keep its monthly buying of Japanese government bonds at one trillion yen ($8.05 billion) and reiterated its pledge to offer even more liquidity in the event of a crisis. \par \par \uc2  Hayami also joined the government in voicing displeasure over a recent series of sovereign debt downgrades by three ratings agencies - Moody's, Standard and Poor's and Fitch. \par \par \uc2  "I find it strange that they are lowering Japan's ratings now," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said Japan had strong fundamentals, including a quality labour force, high technology, and wealth represented by a big current account surplus and high personal savings. \par \par \uc2  "I'm concerned that agencies like Moody's are deciding without taking them into consideration," he said. \par \par \uc2  Smarting at three agency downgrades in six months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  took the unusual step in April of firing off a formal letter of complaint to the main agencies. \par \par \uc2  ($=124.28 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020523dy5n00f06\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
