\par\par \b \uc2  Hayami -  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  was needed, moves too rapid. \b0\par\par\uc2 194 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 08:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 23 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said on Thursday it was good that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  a day earlier in response to rapid moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  But he also said moves over the past few months had reflected the market's view of the relative strength of the Japanese and U.S. economies. \par \par \uc2  "I think it was good that necessary action was taken against the rapid moves," he told a news conference, declining to comment on the effectiveness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or specific exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ left its monetary policy unchanged on Tuesday, maintaining its reserves target at 10-15 trillion yen after a series of easing steps since March 2001 that lowered interest rates to near zero and ensured virtually unlimited funds to the market from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The nine-member board, which also kept policy unchanged at its April meeting, voted unanimously to keep its monthly buying of Japanese government bonds at one trillion yen ($8.05 billion) and reiterated its pledge to offer even more liquidity in the event of a crisis. ($1=124.28 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020523dy5n00czy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
