\par\par \b \uc2  INSTANT VIEW-Japan April trade surplus up 26.6 pct. \b0\par\par\uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 03:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 23 (Reuters) - Following are immediate reactions of analysts and market participants to Japanese trade figures for April released on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's customs-cleared trade surplus rose 26.6 percent from a year earlier to 836.7 billion yen ($6.73 billion), the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  The trade surplus with Japan's largest trading partner, the United States, was down 3.5 percent year-on-year at 646.2 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  FIACHRA MACCANA, HEAD OF EQUITIES RESEARCH AT WEST LB PANMURE \par \par \uc2  "These are pretty much in line. \par \par \uc2  "The story right now in the market is domestic demand, people are looking for a very strong first quarter GDP. People are more optimistic now that consumption is going to turn around. "What we're seeing really now is a catchup by domestic demand sectors like retailers on the back of some fairly decent recent macro information, so this number in itself doesn't start a new trend, it simply confirms that exports are still improving, albeit at a fairly modest pace." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  XINYI LU, CHIEF STRATEGIST, UFJ BANK \par \par \uc2  "I think this was a slightly weaker-than-expected figure so I don't think it will be a factor to buy the yen further. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment for a stronger yen has weakened after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday so I think the impact on the market will be limited." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  MATTHEW POGGI, ECONOMIST, LEHMAN BROTHERS JAPAN INC \par \par \uc2  "I see the surplus continuing to expand in the month ahead. \par \par \uc2  "It's a good sign for the Japanese economy that the trade surplus increase is now being driven by an increase in exports, not just contracting imports. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese economy should get a kick from exports and stabilise in the first half of this year." - - - - \par \par \uc2  SOICHI OKUDA, SENIOR ECONOMIST, AOZORA BANK \par \par \uc2  "The surplus was smaller than expected. But we expect exports to the United States and Asia to remain strong and we think the trade surplus will likely continue to expand. \par \par \uc2  "The yen has been getting stronger on the back of expectations that Japan's trade surplus would grow, but today's weaker-than-expected figure would not provide any support for the yen." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  ROBERT FELDMAN, CHIEF ECONOMIST FOR JAPAN, MORGAN STANLEY \par \par \uc2  "The surplus will begin to moderate at some point relatively soon, partly because of some slowing in U.S. growth and partly because Japanese imports will begin to catch up a bit to the export surge. \par \par \uc2  "But basically, you've got a big trade surplus and it's going to be here, and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets somehow have to absorb the excess dollars. Capital outflows from Japan are not about to accelerate, their capital positions are not strong enough for foreign investment. \par \par \uc2  "There is more attention in the markets to anything that points to a stronger yen, and if this fits the story, fine." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  XINYI LU, CHIEF STRATEGIST, UFJ BANK \par \par \uc2  "I think this was a slightly weaker-than-expected figure so I don't think it will be a factor to buy the yen further. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment for a stronger yen has weakened after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday so I think the impact on the market will be limited." \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  MATTHEW POGGI, ECONOMIST, LEHMAN BROTHERS JAPAN INC \par \par \uc2  "I see the surplus continuing to expand in the month ahead. \par \par \uc2  "It's a good sign for the Japanese economy that the trade surplus increase is now being driven by an increase in exports, not just contracting imports. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese economy should get a kick from exports and stabilise in the first half of this year." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020523dy5n002c3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
