\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 4-Japan export rise supports rosier economic view. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 11:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 23 (Reuters) - Japan's exports rose for the first time in 13 months in the year to April, raising hopes that rebounding shipments of cars and electronic goods would boost other parts of the fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  Weak domestic demand kept imports down, resulting in a 26.6 percent rise in the trade surplus to 836.7 billion yen ($6.73 billion), although that was smaller than the 1.045 trillion yen expected by economists, Finance Ministry data on Thursday showed. \par \par \uc2  Japan's brightening economic outlook has nudged the yen up about 2.5 percent against the dollar in a week as global portfolio flows start to favour Japan and foreign buying of Japanese stocks picks up. \par \par \uc2  Hoping to prevent the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from derailing nascent export-led economic growth, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a second day in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb the yen's strength, driving it back to 124.80 per dollar from around 124. \par \par \uc2  After three consecutive quarters of contraction last year, Japan's gross domestic product (GDP) is widely forecast to have risen in the first quarter of 2002, largely thanks to exports. \par \par \uc2  "We took appropriate action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today just like yesterday," said an official at the Finance Ministry, which uses the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as its agent in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Both the government and Bank of Japan (BOJ) have upgraded their views on the economy for three straight months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  saying this week that the pace of deterioration in the economy had moderated, with production starting to pick up. \par \par \uc2  Unlike the BOJ, the government has declared that the bottom of the recession has been hit, but Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said on Thursday that their views broadly coincided. \par \par \uc2  "We do not use the phrase 'hitting bottom' at the Bank of Japan," Hayami told a news conference. "But I don't think there's a huge difference between (the assessments of) the government and the BOJ." \par \par \uc2  Thanks to yen weakness last year and resilient U.S. demand, Japanese exporters such as automakers have seen their profits rebound despite the recession-bound home economy. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top automaker, Toyota Motor Corp, this month announced record earnings for 2001/02 while Mitsubishi Motors Corp reported a profit for the first time in three years, rounding off a strong earnings season for Japan's carmakers. \par \par \uc2  GLOBAL RECOVERY \par \par \uc2  Japan's exports climbed 1.6 percent in April from a year earlier, although that was below the 3.5 percent rise expected by economists in a Reuters survey. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the trade surplus should narrow soon as Japan's lagging economy recovers. \par \par \uc2  "The surplus will begin to moderate at some point relatively soon, partly because of some slowing in U.S. growth and partly because Japanese imports will begin to catch up a bit to the export surge," said Morgan Stanley chief economist Robert Feldman. \par \par \uc2  Exports to Asia jumped 8.8 percent year-on-year in April, the second straight month of increases, while exports to the United States and Europe continued to decrease year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  In volume terms, exports rose five percent from a year earlier, the first increase in 16 months. \par \par \uc2  "April's exports rose on the back of a recovery in production mainly by manufacturers around the world," a Finance Minisry official told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Imports were down for a ninth straight month, falling 2.9 percent from a year ago, compared with market forecasts for a 5.9 percent drop. \par \par \uc2  On a month-on-month basis, exports rose 0.3 percent from March, while imports jumped 9.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  "It confirms that exports are still improving, albeit at a fairly modest pace," said Fiachra Maccana, head of equities research at West LB Panmure. \par \par \uc2  SPILLOVER \par \par \uc2  A key question has been whether an export-driven recovery in Japan's big manufacturers will spill into the broader economy, which was heavily damaged by the three-quarter-long recession last year, the deepest since World War Two. \par \par \uc2  Consumer spending - which makes up 55 percent of GDP - remains stagnant, although recent surveys suggest consumers are gradually becoming more confident, and employment is showing signs of stabilising. \par \par \uc2  Initial projections are for GDP growth that could be as high as eight or nine percent in January-March on an annualised basis. The figures are released on June 7. \par \par \uc2  . (C) Reuters Limited 2002. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020523dy5n001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
