\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Shiokawa: Sudden Yen Strength A Problem For Econ \b0\par\par\uc2 460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2002\par 10:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as the recent sudden rise in the yen would cause trouble for the recovering domestic economy, Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Such rapid movements in the market will throw everything into confusion," Shiokawa told a regular press conference. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Ministry's yen-selling Wednesday afternoon sent the dollar rocketing above Y125 from a 7-month low of Y123.52 hit earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa added that the ministry acted to regain stability in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, as recent movements had been too rapid. \par \par \uc2  "A movement of five yen or seven yen in one week would cause trouble for anyone," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  "It is important that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are stable," he said. \par \par \uc2  The finance minister said he hadn't given orders for the ministry to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further. The ministry has no plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly with the U.S. or European countries to stem the yen's rise, he said. \par \par \uc2  He also said that the government will aim to drastically reduce general expenditures - the core part of the national budget used for policy-related measures - when drafting next fiscal year's budget. \par \par \uc2  "We will like to maintain the spirit of the Y30 trillion cap," on new government bond issues, Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi has slapped a Y30 trillion limit on new fiscal deficit spending this fiscal year and last to rein in Japan's massive fiscal deficit. \par \par \uc2  But the government is looking to scrap the cap next fiscal year, as maintaining it has grown increasingly unrealistic due to swelling debt-servicing costs. \par \par \uc2  Local media have reported the Finance Ministry will aim to limit next fiscal year's general expenditures at or below the current year's Y47.5 trillion. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said he would like to reduce such general outlays by "a great deal," aiming for around a 10% cut, although he was quick to add that was a ballpark figure and not an official target. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said he hoped to reallocate funds to high-priority growth areas, in order to help revitalize the economy. \par \par \uc2  "We want to review various areas of spending, such as official development assistance and public works," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  Local media have also reported that an advisory panel to Shiokawa's ministry has suggested making a 5%-10% year-on-year reduction in public works spending. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry and Koizumi's top policy-setting panel, the Council on Economic and Fiscal Policy, is expected to draft guidelines next month for next fiscal year's budget. \par \par \uc2  -By Audrey McAvoy and Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; audrey.mcavoy@dowjones.com, miyako.takebe@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020522dy5m000fj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
