\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 :Dollar Soars After  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\uc2  To Cap Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2002\par 09:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar rocketed nearly a yen and a half Wednesday after Japanese monetary authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stop the resurgent yen from snuffing Japan's nascent economic recovery. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry carried out the yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the dollar had tumbled to a seven-month low of Y123.51 earlier in the day in the wake of surprisingly strong Japanese economic data. \par \par \uc2  "Recent movements of exchange rates have been too rapid. We have taken appropriate action today in the exchange market," said Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa in a statement. "We will continue to closely monitor the market and take appropriate action as necessary." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - estimated by some Tokyo traders to have totaled at least $1 billion - sent the dollar flying to a high of Y125.10. Traders said the BOJ started buying dollars between 123.70-80. It wasn't clear if the BOJ had bought any euro against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Deutsche Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Kenneth Landon said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  caught the market off guard, but hasn't changed fundamentals driving the dollar lower. \par \par \uc2  "For now, it's squeezed out some of the speculative moves, so people don't look at it as a one-way bet," Landon said. But "economic fundamentals don't change as a result of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized - not mopping up the extra yen the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold into the market - would do more to reverse the dollar's downward trend, Landon said. \par \par \uc2  "But that remains to be seen. We have to wait to see what the BOJ does," Landon said. \par \par \uc2  All eyes will be on the BOJ's open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday afternoon and Friday morning to see if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  soaks up the yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At 0700 GMT (3:00 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y124.75, above Y124.07 late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was at Y125.95 late Tuesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.9217, higher than $0.9200 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at $0.9182 in Tokyo Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y114.35, up from Y114.14 in New York late Tuesday. The euro was at Y115.65 in Tokyo late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020522dy5m000d9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
